// engine/effects/raster.emp — the sparse HInt raster dispatcher.
//
// Split out of engine/system/hblank.emp (which is dispatch MECHANISM only — the RAM
// jmp-slot trampoline) so the effects suite owns its own modules, per the design's
// file structure. Pure code motion: the program format, the timing model and every
// proc below are byte-for-byte what shipped in chain 105.
//
// Design: docs/superpowers/2026-08-11-effects-suite-design.md §4.
// Timing rules this is built on: docs/research/2026-08-12-raster-hint-survey.md.
module engine.effects.raster in raster

use engine.vdp.{VDP_HINT_OFF, vdp_comm, vdp_comm_addr, VdpTarget, VdpOp}
use engine.constants.{VDP_CTRL, VDP_DATA, SINE_AMPLITUDE, SINE_CYCLE_ENTRIES, SCREEN_HEIGHT}
use engine.irq.{ints_off_until_rte}
// P2 §3.3 capability gate — see the CAP_* authority block in engine/level/scene_dsl.emp.
use engine.level.scene_dsl.{CAP_ANCHORS, CAP_ANCHOR_MOTION}
// The anchor mover's sine source. ONLY THE DATA LABEL is imported — the two sine FACTS come
// in from engine.constants above and are pinned against raster_dsl's literals below; naming
// them through engine.math would yield a LINK EXTERN (a Label) that fails arithmetic, which
// engine/level/parallax.emp measured for the vertical bob and wrote down.
use engine.math.{Sine_Table}

// ===========================================================================
// SPARSE RASTER DISPATCHER (effects P1) — the HBlank trampoline's first consumer.
//
// The SPARSE tier of the two-tier raster engine (design suite §4): a schedule of
// per-scanline VDP work in which HInt fires ONLY on event lines. The dense tier
// (gradient / per-column VSRAM runs) is Phase 2 and arrives as new opcodes.
//
// THE TIMING MODEL (docs/research/2026-08-12-raster-hint-survey.md, Ruling 1b —
// read it before touching this code). The VDP reloads its internal line counter
// from reg $0A *at the instant of underflow*, before the 68000 executes a single
// handler instruction. So a write to $8Axx from inside handler `i` is NOT consumed
// by the gap it is sitting in: it is consumed by the reload at fire `i+1`, and
// therefore schedules gap(i+1 -> i+2). Naive "write next_line - cur_line - 1" is
// off by a whole event.
//
// Two consequences shape the whole format:
//   1. Each fire record carries a BUILD-TIME arm word = $8A00 | (L[i+2]-L[i+1]-1).
//      The runtime never computes a delta and never compares a line number — the
//      schedule IS the program order. (Build-time computation over runtime.)
//   2. The program opens with TWO PRIMING records. Raster_VBlank leaves reg $0A = 0,
//      so cheap no-op fires land on lines 0 and 1 and load the first two real arm
//      words; every fire from the third onward lands exactly on its authored line.
//      Real events therefore start at line >= 2 — lines 0-1 belong to the priming
//      records. State that must cover them is settled state, set with Set_VDP_Reg.
//
// Fire lines are one line EARLY by construction: a register write in the handler
// for fire-line L takes effect from L+1 (Ruling 1a/3a), so an effect authored to
// begin at screen line M is scheduled at fire line M-1. The comptime constructors
// own that -1 so authors think in screen lines.
//
// A compiled raster program is DATA — the Phase-3 `raster_dsl` and Aurora both
// compile to exactly this layout:
//
//   Header — ONE word, so a fire record's arm word is always at word 1
//     dc.w pal_dirty_mask   bits 0-3: palette lines Raster_VBlank re-asserts into
//                           Palette_Dirty EVERY frame, so mid-frame CRAM writes are
//                           transient (the base palette is restored at frame top)
//
//   A program carries NO frame-top register words. It used to: every OP_SET_REG was
//   paired with a reset word in an `init[]` header block that Raster_VBlank replayed at
//   frame top. Flush_VDP_Shadow now re-blits ALL 19 shadowed VDP registers from
//   VDP_Shadow_Table unconditionally every VBlank, which restores every register a
//   mid-frame op touched, for free and without the program having to name it. Deleting
//   the mechanism is what lets two independently-authored effects touch the same register
//   and simply compose, instead of having to agree on a reset word to build at all.
//   A register change meant to SURVIVE the frame is settled state, not a raster op:
//   Set_VDP_Reg (engine/system/vdp_init.emp) writes the shadow and the flush delivers it.
//   Fire records, in FIRE ORDER (first two are the priming no-ops)
//     dc.w arm_word         $8A00 | delta — written at THIS fire, schedules the gap
//                           after next; RASTER_ARM_PARK on the last two real records
//     dc.w op_count         ops to run at this fire (0 = priming / no-op)
//     op_count x { dc.w op; ...args... }
//   Terminator
//     dc.w RASTER_ARM_PARK
//     dc.w RASTER_OPS_END   ($FFFF — park the counter and do NOT advance)
//
// Op argument layouts:
//   OP_SET_REG  dc.w $8xxx                  one VDP register word
//   OP_CRAM     dc.l <vdp_comm CRAM write>   full command longword (ONE move.l)
//               dc.w count-1                 count <= RASTER_BURST_MAX_CRAM
//               dc.w color[count]
//
// CYCLE BUDGET: a CRAM write outside horizontal blanking paints a visible dot
// (single-ported CRAM, Ruling 2b), so the burst ceilings below are a cycle budget and the
// save set is deliberately small — a 4-register movem ROUND TRIP alone is 84 cycles.
// RULING 2a'S "~105 cycles less ~44 for entry, about 60 usable" IS SUPERSEDED and kept
// only to say so: it was a pre-measurement estimate, and the 2026-08-19 sweep measured the
// window at 122.9 cycles (docs/benchmarks/scanline-p2/HBLANK-WINDOW-SWEEP-RESULTS.md).
// Every ceiling here derives from the measured figure; the 60 is what set the retired
// single ceiling of 3, and quoting it now would understate the budget by half.
//
// The movem round trip, derived from
// the 68000 movem timings — register-to-memory predecrement is 8 + 8n and
// memory-to-register postincrement is 12 + 8n, so at n = 4 the push
// (movem.l d0-d1/a1-a2, -(sp)) is 8 + 32 = 40 and the pop is 12 + 32 = 44. This note
// used to say 40, which is the PUSH ONLY — it charged half the round trip it names.
//
// VDP-access invariant (the HBlankHandler note above): the handler runs in raw
// interrupt context and writes the VDP control port, so it can corrupt a two-word
// command latch the main thread was mid-way through. Every main-loop VDP command
// pair reachable while a program is armed must sit inside an `ints_off` bracket.
// The handler itself only ever emits (a) single-word register writes and (b) full
// command longwords in ONE move.l, so it never leaves a split pair of its own.
// ===========================================================================

// ---- opcodes (compare-chain dispatch; see the note in Raster_HInt for why this
// is not a pc-indexed jump table) ----
//
// OP_SET_REG's VALUE IS LOAD-BEARING, not just an ordering. It is 0 so that the op
// fetch's own `move.w (a1)+, d1` decides it: MOVE sets Z from the moved word, so
// Raster_HInt dispatches the commonest op on a flag it already had, with no test
// instruction at all (Tier-3 item 2). Renumbering it to any non-zero value silently
// costs every register write the whole compare chain back — and nothing would fail,
// because the wire-pin lane checks that the model AGREES with these constants, not
// that this one is still zero. Hence the ensure below.
pub const OP_SET_REG    = 0
ensure(OP_SET_REG == 0,
       "OP_SET_REG is {OP_SET_REG}, not 0 — Raster_HInt's `.op_loop` dispatches it on the Z flag that `move.w (a1)+, d1` sets while FETCHING the op, which is only the OP_SET_REG test while the opcode is zero. A non-zero value needs a real compare, and raster_dsl.emp's RASTER_DISPATCH_ZERO_HIT_CYC / _MISS_CYC stop describing the code.")
pub const OP_CRAM       = 2
// OP_PAL_REGION (effects P2, Task 3) — a scanline REGION boundary: stream `count`
// pre-derived colour words from RAM to CRAM. Same 3-word wire body as OP_CRAM but the
// colours come from a RAM SOURCE POINTER (a variant's derived staging bytes) rather
// than being inline in the program. The derive happens at frame time in
// Palette_Compose (Palette_DeriveVariant -> Pal_Variant_Stage); the handler only
// streams, so it stays inside the blanking window the solver aims it at.
//
// WHAT A "REGION" IS (RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP = 3 forces this choice, survey 2a):
// a full 16-colour line CANNOT swap in one fire (3 region words/fire is a cycle budget,
// not a FIFO one). So a region is a SCOPED swap: the few visually-dominant entries of
// a line (<= RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP), streamed in one fire. A full-line region is authored
// as CONSECUTIVE OP_PAL_REGION fires on successive lines, so such a boundary takes
// ceil(N/3) lines to complete. A region op does NOT get the cram class's 4: its burst word
// is the 30-cycle absolute-long form, and four of those leave 2.9 cycles of the window for
// margins that want 30.
//
// THAT IS OUR CHOICE, NOT A REFERENCE'S — an earlier revision of this comment credited it
// to S3K, which is false and was corrected against the disassembly. S3K's HInt3
// (sonic3k.asm:1008-1046) swaps the whole water palette inside ONE interrupt: it parks the
// counter ($8AFF), stops the Z80, then loops {fresh CRAM command + 3 colours, then a
// cycle-counted ~370-cycle dbf spin}. The spacing that pushes the CRAM dots offscreen is
// the SPIN, not multiple interrupts. S3K's HInt2 (:1230), Sonic 2's PalToCRAM and S.C.E.'s
// HInt each write 64 CRAM words in a single fire with no delay at all. Ours trades six
// exception entries for a handler that stays inside the ~60-cycle budget with no Z80 stop
// and no long in-handler spin: the per-op blanking spin below is the same idea as S3K's
// spin at ~40-220 cycles instead of ~370, because it has to cover three words, not sixteen
// colours — and unlike S3K's it is solved per op rather than hand-counted once.
//
// The water cluster (Task 7) uses the scoped form: swap the handful of body colours at
// the water line, not the whole line.
pub const OP_PAL_REGION = 4

// pal_stage_off — the comptime offset into Pal_Variant_Stage an OP_PAL_REGION streams
// from: slot*128 + line*32 + entry*2. The variant staging is PAL_MAX_VARIANTS slots of
// a 128-byte 4-line image each (engine/ram.emp); Palette_DeriveVariant fills the covered
// lines at their natural line*32 offset. Kept as literals (128/32/2) rather than
// importing palette's consts to avoid an engine.effects.raster -> engine.effects.palette
// module edge for two numbers; the ensure() below pins the slot bound.
pub comptime fn pal_stage_off(slot: int, line: int, entry: int) -> int {
    ensure(slot >= 0 && slot < 2,     "pal_stage_off: slot {slot} outside 0..PAL_MAX_VARIANTS(2)")
    ensure(line >= 1 && line <= 3,    "pal_stage_off: line {line} outside 1..3 (line 0 is the character's)")
    ensure(entry >= 0 && entry <= 15, "pal_stage_off: entry {entry} outside 0..15")
    return slot * 128 + line * 32 + entry * 2
}

// OP_RUN_GRADIENT (effects P2, Task 5) — the DENSE tier. A dense run is a MODE, not an
// op that returns: for a line RANGE the handler fires EVERY line (reg $0A=0) and runs a
// minimal per-line body (write a constant CRAM command + 3 stream colours), then restores
// sparse delta dispatch at the end of the run. Wire body:
//   OP_RUN_GRADIENT
//   dc.l <CRAM write command longword>   constant target for every line (e.g. 3 sky entries)
//   dc.w <line count L>
//   dc.l <ROM gradient stream pointer>   L*3 colour words, 3 per line (pre-computed, no
//                                        handler arithmetic; Palette_Compose may refill it
//                                        when the gradient must follow the live palette)
pub const OP_RUN_GRADIENT = 6

// OP_RUN_RAMP — the dense tier's second body, and the first raster effect in this engine
// whose per-line values are COMPUTED rather than baked. Same run mechanics as
// OP_RUN_GRADIENT (fire every line, constant command, count down), but instead of reading
// three words per line from ROM it advances a 16.16 accumulator and writes its integer
// half. Wire body:
//   OP_RUN_RAMP
//   dc.l <VDP write command longword>    constant target for every line
//   dc.w <line count L>
//   dc.l <start>                         16.16 initial accumulator
//   dc.l <step>                          16.16 per-line delta, SIGNED
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. A gradient stream is frozen in ROM: it cannot change without rebuilding
// the data, so every dense effect was a fixture rather than a preset. The accumulator's
// two words live in RAM (Raster_Ramp_Acc / Raster_Ramp_Step) and a game may rewrite them
// on any frame, which is what turns "a gradient" into "a parameterised effect" — the
// dynamic-uniforms half the vocabulary review named as missing.
//
// Pointed at VSRAM this is vertical scale: each line scrolls the plane by a different
// amount, giving squash, stretch, and the perspective-floor family. Five reference
// engines independently converged on this exact 16.16-accumulator shape (Ristar's floor
// at ROM $061ACE does one divu per frame then add/asr per line; Vectorman's scaler at
// $0006F6F8 uses no table at all; also Gunstar, Alien Soldier and Thunder Force IV), and
// none of them could parameterise it from a data file the way this can.
pub const OP_RUN_RAMP = 8

// OP_PAL_RESTORE (Parcel R1) — the band's OFF edge: stream `count` base colours from
// Palette_Ship_Snap (this frame's base-DMA payload) back to CRAM.
//   dc.l <CRAM write command longword>    target, derived from the same addr field
//   dc.w count-1                          count <= RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP
//   dc.w addr                             snapshot offset == CRAM byte address
// Appended LAST for two invariants, neither of them body bytes: (1) .op_region keeps
// dispatch DEPTH 1, which the spin solver prices explicitly — renumbering would silently
// move every region op's landing, since depth is one of the solver's inputs; (2) F1=396
// proved the chain assembles byte-displacement Bcc — a mid-chain insertion risks a
// silent word-relaxation (+4/rung) failing the pins while blaming the cost model.
// The SetReg fall-through pays the +16 rung tax (F1 396->412, F5 612->628, re-pinned
// in raster_dsl.emp by the encoder task). specs/2026-08-16-parcel-r1-palette-bands-v6.md §3.
pub const OP_PAL_RESTORE = 10

// op_count sentinel: park the counter and do not advance the cursor. Reached only
// by a stray fire (a counter that somehow wrapped), so it is defense, not flow.
pub const RASTER_OPS_END = $FFFF

// reg $0A arm word that parks the counter past the end of the frame ($8A00|255):
// the next fire would be 256 lines away, i.e. never within active display.
pub const RASTER_ARM_PARK = $8AFF

// reg $0A arm word for the dense tier: value 0 = fire EVERY line (Task 5).
pub const RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE = $8A00

// The first V-counter line OUTSIDE active display — the frame-rewind interlock's whole
// discriminator (Raster_HInt `.priming`; read that arm for why the test has to be the beam).
// Named rather than inlined because it is one half of a two-sided fact and the other half is
// RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE; the pin below is what keeps them from drifting apart.
pub const RASTER_VBLANK_V = 224

// ---- the per-fire burst ceilings — a CYCLE budget, and one PER CLASS ---------
// Words a single fire may stream. NOT a FIFO or CRAM limit (Ruling 2b's dot is about
// WHERE a write lands, not how many): the whole burst has to fit inside one H40
// horizontal blanking, measured at 122.9 cycles, with the solver's margins at both ends.
//
// THERE ARE TWO OF THESE BECAUSE A BURST WORD COSTS TWO DIFFERENT AMOUNTS. `.cram_loop`
// still holds VDP_CTRL in a2 when it reaches its burst, so it writes through `-4(a2)` and
// a word is 26 cycles; `.region_loop` and `.restore_loop` have spent a2 on their SOURCE
// cursor (Pal_Variant_Stage / Palette_Ship_Snap), so their write is the absolute-long form
// and a word is 30 (substrate Tier-3 item 1, and see the note beside those loops for why
// freeing a2 loses). A single ceiling would have to be one of: 4, which no deep burst can
// place, or 3, which prices the cheap class as if it were the dear one — the same
// one-number-for-two-facts defect the retired `RASTER_SPIN_CRAM` was.
//
// THE ARITHMETIC EACH ONE ANSWERS. A burst of n words spans word_cyc * (n-1) from its
// first write to its last; the solver wants that span plus both margins (20 early + 10
// late, raster_dsl.emp) inside 122.9:
//
//   class            n=3                    n=4                     n=5
//   cram/vsram (26)  52 + 30 = 82           78 + 30 = 108           104 + 30 = 134  REFUSED
//   region/restore   60 + 30 = 90           90 + 30 = 120  (2.9     120 + 30 = 150  REFUSED
//   (30)                                    cyc of slack — under
//                                           the instrument's own
//                                           +-5, i.e. not distinct
//                                           from zero)
//
// THAT TABLE IS THE ARITHMETIC AND IT IS NOT THE WHOLE ANSWER. It asks only whether the span
// plus both margins FITS the window; it does not ask where the solver's rounding actually
// puts the burst. The cram n=4 row has 14.9 cycles of arithmetic slack and still lost, because
// the spin quantizes to whole `dbf` iterations and the nearest one leaves 0.9 cycles at the
// early edge. A fit check is necessary, not sufficient — see the measured block below.
//
// AND THE CRAM ROW WAS MEASURED — WHICH IS WHY IT IS 3 AND NOT 4.
// tools/hblank_window_sweep.py authored a 4-word leading OP_CRAM directly in wire (the
// constructors below refuse it) and swept its spin across four sampling periods, three burst
// widths, twelve groups. The burst itself behaved exactly as the table says: the first word
// crosses at the same instant whatever the width (the control that says a wider burst does
// not START later), and the spans track the 26-cycle word to inside two cycles.
//
// What refused the raise was the MARGIN, once the window anchor was re-derived. At
// RASTER_HBLANK_END_CYC = 366 the solver gives a 4-word cram burst 18 iterations, which puts
// its first write 20.9 cycles after the start of blanking against an early margin of 20 —
// **0.9 cycles of slack**, with the pooled estimator's own standard error at 2.0. The late
// side is comfortable (+14.0); the early side is not, and the early side is the DERIVED edge,
// the one no instrument here can watch. A ceiling whose placement survives by nine tenths of
// a cycle is one pre-burst instruction away from making a legal program unbuildable.
//
//   width  solved spin  early slack  late slack     (at END = 366, margins 20 / 10)
//     3        19          +10.9       +30.0        shipped
//     4        18           +0.9       +14.0        REFUSED — 0.9 cyc, s.e. 2.0
//     5        17           -9.1        -2.0        REFUSED — unplaceable, both edges
//
// THE RULE THIS WAS DECIDED BY, fixed before the number was known so it could not be argued
// with afterwards: the raise stands only if the binding margin clears BOTH two standard
// errors of the pooled estimator (4.0 cyc) and the 2.9-cycle threshold the DEEP class was
// refused on. 0.9 clears neither. Holding here is the same standard applied to both classes,
// which is the only way either refusal means anything.
//
// SO WHY ARE THERE STILL TWO CONSTANTS AT THE SAME VALUE? Because they are two different
// facts that happen to agree today, and the code has to keep them apart to move either one:
// their word costs differ (26 vs 30), their placeability ensures are separate, and the cram
// class is the one with headroom — 14.9 cycles of arithmetic slack against the deep class's
// 2.9. A wider window, a cheaper pre-burst path, or an instrument that can see the early edge
// unlocks 4 for cram alone, and that is a one-token change to the line below.
// docs/benchmarks/scanline-p2/HBLANK-WINDOW-SWEEP-RESULTS.md, "4-word CRAM burst".
pub const RASTER_BURST_MAX_CRAM = 3
pub const RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP = 3
ensure(RASTER_BURST_MAX_CRAM >= RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP,
       "RASTER_BURST_MAX_CRAM ({RASTER_BURST_MAX_CRAM}) is below RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP ({RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP}) — the cram class has the CHEAPER burst word (26 vs 30), so it can never afford fewer words than the deep class. One of the two has been edited against its cycle class.")

// The dense tier's three writes per line — `.dense_body`'s literal
// `move.w (a1)+, -4(a2)` x3, and the stride `raster_gradient_program` sizes its stream by.
// IT IS NOT A CEILING and it never was: nothing solves a spin for it, nothing checks it
// against the blanking window, and raising it means editing the handler. It wore
// RASTER_CRAM_MAX's name until the ceilings split per class, which made one constant carry
// three unrelated facts; this is the third, given its own name so that moving a ceiling
// cannot silently resize a ROM stream.
pub const RASTER_DENSE_WORDS_PER_LINE = 3

// ---- row-119 partial-tint fix (Task 4) — DECIDED, measured on oracle 2026-08-13 ----
// The P1 residual: a CRAM write lands inside the fire+1 line's ACTIVE display, so pixels
// drawn after the write on that line carry the new colour (single-ported CRAM, survey
// Ruling 2b). Two options were built behind a select and measured at an IDENTICAL
// camera (2840, 356), section 1, both shapes converged:
//
//   (a) fire one line early — REJECTED and DELETED. It never removed the artifact by
//       construction (it relocates the partial row from M-1 to M-2 and starts the whole
//       effect a line high), and the audit found its helper `raster_fire_screen` was
//       imported but NEVER CALLED, so the option was dead code that had never affected
//       a shipped program. Numbers recorded in docs/benchmarks/effects-p2/GATE-EVIDENCE.md
//       so it is not re-litigated.
//   (b) blanking delay — ADOPTED, and now unconditional. A cycle-counted dbf before each
//       CRAM write parks it in HBlank (offscreen). Measured: row 119 goes 1 px -> 0 px
//       and the boundary lands on the AUTHORED line 120 instead of 119. S3K's approach
//       (sonic3k.asm:1018, :1038-1039, retuned per region, runtime-selected by measured
//       VBlank length at :9791-9793).
//
// RESIDUAL, deliberately not fixed here: the delay guards the CRAM paths only, so a fire
// mixing OP_SET_REG with a CRAM op still switches its mode REGISTER mid-line. Measured at
// ~45% across line 119 (column-bucket brightness, identical in both options). Authors
// wanting a pixel-clean mode change must schedule it a line earlier; extending the delay
// to OP_SET_REG would cost another ~40 cycles of a ~60-cycle budget and was not taken
// without a cycle measurement to justify it.
//
// THE SPIN IS NO LONGER A CONSTANT HERE. It is a comptime-solved word carried by each
// stream op in the program, read by the three delay sites below with `move.w (a1)+, d1`.
// The authority is `fire_spins` / `solve_spin` in engine/effects/raster_dsl.emp.
//
// WHY THE CONSTANTS DIED (substrate sweep item 1, 2026-08-18). `EFX_BLANK_DELAY = 4` was
// fitted to ONE shape: a CRAM op sitting SECOND, after an OP_SET_REG, which contributes
// 110 cycles of pre-burst delay ahead of it. The same 4 applied to a LEADING stream op —
// a shape `band(sh: 0)`, `region_boundary(sh: 0)` and `fx_tint_band(sh: 0)` all freely
// produce — left it ~150 cycles early, landing at x~170 of 320, i.e. mid-active-display.
// R1's §7.3 capture measured exactly that ("the bare single-op cram ON fire spilled
// identically ... row 99 tinted from x~170"). A constant cannot be right for both shapes
// because the distance to blanking depends on the op's POSITION and DISPATCH DEPTH, which
// only the lowering knows. So the lowering computes it.
//
// AND SINCE ITEM 1c THE SOLVER IS REAL (2026-08-19). The three per-CLASS anchors that
// stood in for the constants (4/4/13) are gone too; the spin is centred in a blanking
// window MEASURED on hardware, and the retired values are now REPRODUCED as facts about
// that window rather than fed into it: 13 is the latest spin whose 3-word restore burst
// still beats the sampling instant, and 4 lands the reg-prefixed cram 2.1 cycles (1.8 px)
// short of the window opening — which is the '1px -> 0px' the P2 gate measured on that
// exact fixture. Both are pinned in raster_dsl.emp beside the window constant.

// Working-buffer size (bytes). Buf_B is the Phase-2 patch/double-buffer seam;
// P1 runs entirely out of Buf_A.
pub const RASTER_BUF_SIZE = 128

// Raster_State span guard — the RAM block (engine/ram.emp) is the twin of this
// layout; a drift on either side must fail the build, not the frame.
//   Program 4 + Cursor 4 + Pending 4 + Line 2 + Buf_A + Buf_B + Active_Buf 4
//   + Dense_Lines 2 + Dense_Cursor 4 + Dense_Cmd 4
//   + Dense_Kind 2 + Ramp_Acc 4 + Ramp_Step 4
//   + Effects_World_Y (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH) + Effects_Screen_L (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH)
//   + Effects_Motion (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH) + Effects_Target (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH)
//   + Effects_RateMask (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH) + Effects_Motion_Any 2
//   + Effects_Offscreen_Entry 4 + Patch_Tab 4
//
// THIS ensure IS WHAT HOLDS ram.emp's LITERAL 4 TO RASTER_MAX_PATCH. ram.emp is built by a
// separate ram-harvest pass outside the COMPTIME_HELPERS glob injection, so it cannot NAME
// the constant and spells `[u16; 4]` instead. Nothing there can catch a drift — but this
// measures the real emitted span between the two marks, so raising RASTER_MAX_PATCH without
// widening the RAM array fails the build HERE, which is the whole reason the span guard is
// phrased against extern() marks rather than against a hand-added byte count.
pub const RASTER_STATE_SIZE = 4 + 4 + 4 + 2 + RASTER_BUF_SIZE + RASTER_BUF_SIZE + 4 + 2 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 4 + 4 + (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH) + (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH) + (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH) + (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH) + (2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH) + 2 + 4 + 4
ensure((extern("Raster_State_End") - extern("Raster_State")) == RASTER_STATE_SIZE,
       "Raster_State RAM block drifted from hblank.emp's raster layout")

// ---- THE ANCHOR MOVER'S LADDER PINS (EFFECTS-W1 DoD item 4) ----------------
//
// engine/effects/raster_dsl.emp derives ANCHOR_SWEEP_SHIFT_MIN / _MAX / _PERIOD_SHIFT_MAX /
// _PHASE_MAX from three LITERALS, because a COMPTIME_HELPERS module's `pub const` must fold
// from its own file's names (that module says why, and engine/level/parallax.emp measured the
// failure). This is where those ladders meet the constants they are supposed to be derived
// FROM: an ORDINARY module, where an imported name in a module-scope `ensure` is evaluated
// once in its own scope instead of being glob-injected into every module's stream.
//
// The pins RE-DERIVE rather than compare to a copy — each states the bracketing property that
// makes the ladder rung the LAST legal one, so a change to the sine table or the screen height
// fails here naming the new numbers, and a hand-edited ladder fails here too.
ensure((SINE_AMPLITUDE >> ANCHOR_SWEEP_SHIFT_MIN) * 2 <= SCREEN_HEIGHT && (SINE_AMPLITUDE >> (ANCHOR_SWEEP_SHIFT_MIN - 1)) * 2 > SCREEN_HEIGHT,
       "the anchor sweep's widest rung ({ANCHOR_SWEEP_SHIFT_MIN}) is not the smallest shift whose peak-to-peak travel (2 * ({SINE_AMPLITUDE} >> shift)) fits a {SCREEN_HEIGHT}-line screen. raster_dsl.emp derives it from its own literals ({SINE_AMPLITUDE} / {SCREEN_HEIGHT} spelled there); one of those literals has drifted from engine.constants, or the ladder was edited by hand. A sweep wider than the screen cannot fit ANY patchable band, so the edge would be deleted at the top of every cycle rather than moving")
ensure((SINE_AMPLITUDE >> ANCHOR_SWEEP_SHIFT_MAX) >= 1 && (SINE_AMPLITUDE >> (ANCHOR_SWEEP_SHIFT_MAX + 1)) == 0,
       "the anchor sweep's narrowest rung ({ANCHOR_SWEEP_SHIFT_MAX}) is not the last shift that leaves the sine table a nonzero positive peak against amplitude {SINE_AMPLITUDE}. One past it, `asr.w` returns 0 across the whole positive half-cycle and -1 across the negative one: a 1-px DC droop and no motion, which reads as a broken sweep rather than a subtle one")
ensure((SINE_CYCLE_ENTRIES << ANCHOR_SWEEP_PERIOD_SHIFT_MAX) <= 65536 && (SINE_CYCLE_ENTRIES << (ANCHOR_SWEEP_PERIOD_SHIFT_MAX + 1)) > 65536,
       "the anchor sweep's slowest rung ({ANCHOR_SWEEP_PERIOD_SHIFT_MAX}) is not the last period shift whose cycle ({SINE_CYCLE_ENTRIES} << shift ticks) still closes inside the 16-bit Logic_Tick low word the mover reads. One further and the phase index has reached only part of the table when the counter wraps, and the sine jumps by its full amplitude at that instant")
// ---- THE TWO RAM ADJACENCIES Effects_LatchWorldLines ADDRESSES THROUGH ------
//
// The mover reaches four banks with TWO address registers, and it has to: three separate
// `lea`s put it on a3, which Effects_InstallPreset holds the live EffectsPreset* in across its
// call to this proc — sigil's [call.live-clobbered] closure reported exactly that, measured on
// this parcel's first build. So one cursor walks Motion / Target / RateMask by displacement,
// and the screen bank is reached as `6(a0)` off the post-incremented anchor cursor.
//
// BOTH ARE FACTS ABOUT engine/ram.emp'S DECLARATION ORDER, WHICH THIS FILE CANNOT SEE. An
// unrelated field inserted between any two of them would not fail to build — it would write
// screen lines into the wrong bank and read sweep words out of the anchors, silently. These
// are the guards that turn that into a build error naming the field that moved. They are
// phrased against `extern()` marks rather than a hand-added byte count for the same reason
// RASTER_STATE_SIZE's is.
ensure((extern("Effects_Screen_L") - extern("Effects_World_Y")) == 2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH,
       "Effects_Screen_L is no longer 2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH bytes after Effects_World_Y, so Effects_LatchWorldLines' motion arm — which stores this channel's screen line as `6(a0)` off the post-incremented anchor cursor rather than spending a second address register on it — writes into whatever now sits between them. Either put the two banks back adjacent in engine/ram.emp, or give the motion arm its own screen cursor and pay the register (the comment at the `lea Sine_Table, a1` says what that costs)")
ensure((extern("Effects_Target") - extern("Effects_Motion")) == 2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH && (extern("Effects_RateMask") - extern("Effects_Motion")) == 4 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH,
       "the anchor mover's three banks (Effects_Motion, Effects_Target, Effects_RateMask) are no longer three adjacent [u16; RASTER_MAX_PATCH] arrays in engine/ram.emp. Effects_LatchWorldLines walks all three from ONE cursor as `(a2)`, `8(a2)` and `16(a2)`, because three `lea`s would need a3 and Effects_InstallPreset holds the EffectsPreset* there across the call. A field inserted between them does not fail to build — it makes the ramp read sweep words as targets")
ensure(ANCHOR_SWEEP_PHASE_MAX == SINE_CYCLE_ENTRIES - 1,
       "the anchor sweep's phase range ({ANCHOR_SWEEP_PHASE_MAX}) is not one full cycle of the sine table ({SINE_CYCLE_ENTRIES} entries). The mover folds the phase in by ADDING the packed motion word to the shifted tick and masking with {SINE_CYCLE_ENTRIES}-1, so a phase that overflowed the low byte would corrupt the amplitude and period nibbles instead of wrapping")

// ---- comptime authoring helpers -------------------------------------------
// What survives here is the arm arithmetic the DENSE tier still authors with: the
// arm word at record i schedules gap(i+1 -> i+2). The SPARSE tier's authoring
// surface — including the other off-by-one, `fire line = screen line - 1` — is
// engine.effects.raster_dsl, which owns the general constructors and their
// validation. Helpers this module no longer needs have been deleted, not kept
// dormant (raster_fire_screen at the row-119 note above set that precedent).

// raster_arm — the arm word to place in record i, given the FIRE lines of records
// i+1 and i+2. Pass RASTER_ARM_PARK's operand (255) when there is no i+2.
pub comptime fn raster_arm(next_fire: int, after_fire: int) -> int {
    ensure(after_fire > next_fire,
           "raster_arm: fire lines must strictly increase ({next_fire} -> {after_fire})")
    ensure(after_fire - next_fire - 1 <= 255,
           "raster_arm: gap {next_fire}->{after_fire} exceeds one counter reload")
    return $8A00 | (after_fire - next_fire - 1)
}

// ---- dense-run authoring (Task 5) -----------------------------------------
// RasterGradientProgram — the wire shape of a minimal dense-run program: two priming
// records, the setup record carrying OP_RUN_GRADIENT, and the terminator. A struct
// rather than the flat [u16] the sparse fixtures use, because the gradient op carries a
// LINK-TIME stream pointer — OP_PAL_REGION deliberately avoided one by carrying a
// comptime staging offset instead, but a ROM stream has no staging base to be relative
// to, and a [u16] literal array cannot hold a symbol address.
//
// THE ENTER SCHEDULE, for a run of L lines whose first stream line lands on screen line
// T (the other half of the mode-switch schedule commented at the dense fast path):
//   fire 0 @ line 0     priming, arm = raster_arm(1, T-1)
//   fire 1 @ line 1     priming, arm = raster_arm(T-1, T) = the every-line word
//   fire 2 @ line T-1   setup: OP_RUN_GRADIENT stashes cmd/lines/cursor, writes nothing
//   fire 3 @ line T     first .dense_body -> stream[0] shows on screen line T
//   ...                 L dense fires; the last stream line lands on screen line T+L-1
// Record i's arm governs gap(i+1 -> i+2), so BOTH record 1 and record 2 must carry the
// every-line word: record 1's puts fire 3 at T, record 2's puts fire 4 at T+1. The
// setup fire writes no colours of its own, which is why it gets its own line at T-1.
//
// THE T-1 SETUP LINE IS MEASURED, NOT DERIVED. The naive derivation puts the setup at
// T-2 (fire 3 then lands at T-1, whose handler write shows on T, per the sparse tier's
// fire-line = M-1 rule). On hardware that authors the whole run ONE LINE HIGH: with
// top=96 the level boundaries came out at 107/119/131/143/155/167/179 instead of
// 108/120/.../180 — a uniform -1 across all seven, i.e. stream[0] displayed on 95.
// The dense path does not inherit the sparse -1 because entering the run costs its own
// pipelined arm (Ruling 1b at the ENTER edge), which absorbs exactly one line. Evidence:
// docs/benchmarks/effects-p2/GATE-EVIDENCE.md, dense-tier section.
pub struct RasterGradientProgram {
    rgp_mask:       u16,    // pal_dirty_mask — DERIVED from rgp_cmd's CRAM address
    rgp_arm0:       u16, rgp_ops0: u16,     // fire 0 — priming
    rgp_arm1:       u16, rgp_ops1: u16,     // fire 1 — priming
    rgp_arm2:       u16, rgp_ops2: u16,     // fire 2 — the setup record, one op
    rgp_op:         u16,
    rgp_cmd:        u32,    // constant CRAM write command, re-issued every dense line
    rgp_lines:      u16,
    rgp_stream:     *u8,    // rgp_lines * RASTER_DENSE_WORDS_PER_LINE colour words
    rgp_end_arm:    u16, rgp_end_ops: u16,  // terminator
}

// raster_gradient_program — build the schedule above. `cmd` is the CRAM write command
// longword, passed in already built (game-side, pinned against engine.vdp's comptime
// builder).
//
// THE MASK IS DERIVED, NOT ACCEPTED. It used to be a parameter, and it was the last
// hand-authorable pal_dirty_mask in the suite — the one field with a shipped history of
// producing wrong pixels (P1: the mask said %0001 while the op wrote line 2, so the red
// covered the whole ground instead of the band, permanently). The sparse tier has always
// derived it (raster_dsl's op_mask); the dense tier took the author's word for it. Now
// both derive from the same fact — the CRAM address inside the command longword — so a
// mask that disagrees with the write is UNREPRESENTABLE rather than merely detectable.
//
// `stream_words` is the array's actual length, checked against the run's demand. The
// alternative was a co-located hand-written ensure at each call site (the shipped fixture
// carried one), which every future dense program would have to remember to copy — the
// ritual this codebase keeps discovering it has not copied.
//
// THERE IS NO `init_word` PARAMETER ANY MORE. A dense program used to carry one frame-top
// register word (defaulting to boot's H40 base $8C81) that Raster_VBlank replayed; the
// unconditional Flush_VDP_Shadow now restores every shadowed register at frame top, so the
// header word had nothing left to do. A section that wants a register CHANGED globally —
// Shadow/Highlight permanently on, say — calls Set_VDP_Reg (engine/system/vdp_init.emp)
// instead: it writes the shadow byte, and the frame-top flush is what delivers it.
pub comptime fn raster_gradient_program(top: int, lines: int, cmd: int,
                                        stream: Label, stream_words: int) -> RasterGradientProgram {
    // Floor is 3, matching `raster_dsl.fire`: the setup fire sits at T-1 and must still
    // land after the two priming fires on lines 0-1.
    ensure(top >= 3, "raster_gradient_program: top {top} below 3 (priming needs lines 0-1)")
    ensure(lines >= 1, "raster_gradient_program: line count {lines} must be positive")
    // THE CEILING IS 223, NOT 224, AND IT STAYS — but NOT for the reason it was added.
    //
    // WHAT IT WAS ADDED FOR, AND WHY THAT DERIVATION IS WRONG. The original argument ran: the
    // HInt counter decrements for lines 0..224, so `top + lines == 224` is the authorable case
    // that puts a fire on line 223, "the same line VINT fires"; IRQ6 masks level 4, so that
    // IRQ4 defers past Raster_VBlank's rewind, consumes priming record 0 and shifts the whole
    // next frame. Source-confirmed, never measured. It has now been measured
    // (tools/raster_frame_epoch_probe.py, 2026-08-19) and the middle step does not hold:
    // fires authored at 222, at 223 and at 224 ALL retire before the rewind, every frame, with
    // an identical [2,6,10] cursor walk — including the dense run 220..223, i.e. exactly
    // `top + lines == 224`. The VDP raises the last active line's HINT far enough ahead of
    // VINT that the 68000 takes IRQ4 first. This bound was never what stood between the engine
    // and the hazard.
    //
    // WHAT DOES. The real precondition is the CPU sitting at IPL >= 4 across the VINT instant
    // with an IRQ4 already raised, which is a MASK WINDOW and not a line number — reachable
    // from any `ints_off` bracket straddling the end of active display, at any authored line.
    // The interlock that closes it is in `Raster_HInt`'s `.priming` arm (read it for the
    // mechanism and the measurements); docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md's substrate item 3 is closed
    // there, structurally, not here.
    //
    // SO WHY KEEP 223? Because the interlock's discriminator IS the line: it retires any fire
    // reaching `.priming` with V >= RASTER_VBLANK_V (224) as stale. A run authored to put a
    // fire on 224 would be authoring real work into the region the interlock treats as stale.
    // The bound and the threshold are one fact, pinned together by the `ensure` beside
    // RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE. Relaxing it to 224 is therefore NOT a free follow-up: it would
    // have to move the threshold too, and the threshold has nowhere to go — 224 is where
    // active display ends. Booked as such rather than left looking like slack.
    //
    // Nothing shipped is near it either way: the two callers in the tree are OJZ_TestGradient
    // (96+96 = 192) and OJZ_TestRamp (112+96 = 208).
    //
    // A literal 223 and not a named constant ON PURPOSE. This is a comptime fn body, so its
    // free names resolve at the EMISSION site's scope (the import note at the head of
    // games/sonic4/data/effects/ojz_effects.emp documents the rule and its failure modes).
    // A new name here would have to be imported by every present and future call site, and a
    // degraded name that collides with a real label or `equ` is the one SILENT case — which in
    // a guard means the guard stops guarding. The literal cannot degrade.
    ensure(top + lines <= 223,
           "raster_gradient_program: run {top}..{top + lines - 1} must END at screen line 222 or above. The last dense line's LEAVE schedule puts a trailing fire one line below it, and the frame-rewind interlock (Raster_HInt's `.priming` arm) retires as stale any fire it reaches with the V counter at or past RASTER_VBLANK_V — so a run ending at 223 would be authoring work into the region the interlock treats as post-rewind. The bound and that threshold are one fact, pinned together beside RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE. Shorten the run: see the note above this ensure, including why the ORIGINAL derivation for this bound was measured and found wrong.")
    // The command must BE a CRAM write, or the derived mask is a palette line computed
    // from something that is not a palette address. The round trip proves target, op and
    // address in one comparison, and it subsumes the pin each call site used to carry.
    let addr = vdp_comm_addr(cmd)
    ensure(cmd == vdp_comm(addr, VdpTarget.Cram, VdpOp.Write),
           "raster_gradient_program: cmd {cmd} is not a CRAM WRITE command — the dense run streams colour words to CRAM and derives its pal_dirty_mask from this address, so a VRAM or VSRAM command here would both write the wrong place and re-assert a palette line computed from a non-palette address")
    ensure(addr <= 126,
           "raster_gradient_program: CRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..126")
    ensure((addr >> 5) != 0,
           "raster_gradient_program: the run writes CRAM line 0, the CHARACTER's palette line — a dense run re-asserts its line every frame, so this would fight the player's palette for the whole section")
    ensure(stream_words == lines * RASTER_DENSE_WORDS_PER_LINE,
           "raster_gradient_program: stream is {stream_words} words but the run needs lines * RASTER_DENSE_WORDS_PER_LINE = {lines * RASTER_DENSE_WORDS_PER_LINE}. A short stream is read past its end into whatever ROM follows, for the rest of the run, every frame.")
    return RasterGradientProgram{
        rgp_mask:       1 << (addr >> 5),
        rgp_arm0:       raster_arm(1, top - 1),
        rgp_ops0:       0,
        rgp_arm1:       raster_arm(top - 1, top),       // == RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE
        rgp_ops1:       0,
        rgp_arm2:       RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE,
        rgp_ops2:       1,
        rgp_op:         OP_RUN_GRADIENT,
        rgp_cmd:        cmd,
        rgp_lines:      lines,
        rgp_stream:     stream,
        rgp_end_arm:    RASTER_ARM_PARK,
        rgp_end_ops:    RASTER_OPS_END,
    }
}

// RasterRampProgram — the wire shape of a dense RAMP program. Same schedule as
// RasterGradientProgram (two priming records, a setup record carrying the op, then the
// terminator); the body differs only in what the setup record carries.
pub struct RasterRampProgram {
    rrp_mask:       u16,    // pal_dirty_mask — see the constructor; 0 for a non-CRAM target
    rrp_arm0:       u16, rrp_ops0: u16,     // fire 0 — priming
    rrp_arm1:       u16, rrp_ops1: u16,     // fire 1 — priming
    rrp_arm2:       u16, rrp_ops2: u16,     // fire 2 — the setup record, one op
    rrp_op:         u16,
    rrp_cmd:        u32,    // constant VDP write command, re-issued every line
    rrp_lines:      u16,
    rrp_start:      u32,    // 16.16 initial accumulator
    rrp_step:       u32,    // 16.16 per-line delta, signed
    rrp_end_arm:    u16, rrp_end_ops: u16,  // terminator
}

// raster_ramp_program — a dense run whose per-line value is COMPUTED from a 16.16
// accumulator instead of streamed from ROM, which is what makes it parameterisable at
// runtime (Raster_Ramp_Acc / Raster_Ramp_Step are RAM).
//
// `start` and `step` are 16.16 FIXED POINT: 65536 is one unit per line. Authors should
// use fp16() below rather than writing the raw longword, so the scale is stated once.
//
// WHICH LINE THE FIRST VALUE LANDS ON DEPENDS ON THE TARGET, and it is MEASURED, not
// assumed (docs/benchmarks/effects-p3/RAMP-EVIDENCE.md, 74 of 74 rows):
//   CRAM target  — value j displays on screen line top + j     (like the gradient tier)
//   VSRAM target — value j displays on screen line top + j + 1
// The extra line is the same N+1 behaviour measured for a sparse `stream_vsram` write on
// 2026-08-14: a VSRAM write issued in line N's HBlank takes effect on N+1, while CRAM
// applies to the line being set up. So a VSRAM ramp's `top` line renders with whatever
// scroll was live BEFORE the run, and the ramp proper starts one line down.
//
// This is deliberately NOT corrected by biasing `top` inside the constructor. The bias
// would have to be conditional on the target, which puts a silent one-line shift between
// two calls that differ only in their command word — the exact class of surprise the
// sparse tier's single `-1` rule was designed to avoid. Author the line you want the
// ramp to START at and, for VSRAM, expect the line above it to hold the previous value.
//
// THE MASK IS DERIVED, exactly as the gradient constructor derives it, and for the same
// reason — a pal_dirty_mask naming the wrong line leaves a mid-frame write latched
// forever. A ramp pointed at VSRAM writes no palette, so its mask is 0; that is the same
// ruling `stream_vsram`'s op_mask carries in the sparse tier, and it is NOT an omission: a
// CRAM-style `1 << (addr >> 5)` computed from a VSRAM address is a palette line derived
// from a scroll offset, and at offset 0 it names the CHARACTER's line.
//
// THERE IS NO `init_word` PARAMETER ANY MORE, for the same reason the gradient constructor
// lost its one: Flush_VDP_Shadow re-blits every shadowed register from VDP_Shadow_Table at
// the top of every VBlank, so a program-carried frame-top word is redundant. A section
// wanting a globally-changed register (Shadow/Highlight permanently on, for instance)
// calls Set_VDP_Reg (engine/system/vdp_init.emp) and lets the flush deliver it.
pub comptime fn raster_ramp_program(top: int, lines: int, cmd: int,
                                    start: int, step: int) -> RasterRampProgram {
    ensure(top >= 3, "raster_ramp_program: top {top} below 3 (priming needs lines 0-1)")
    ensure(lines >= 1, "raster_ramp_program: line count {lines} must be positive")
    // THE CEILING IS 223, NOT 224 — the full note is above raster_gradient_program's matching
    // ensure and applies verbatim here, including the part where the ORIGINAL derivation for
    // this bound (a fire on 223 raising HINT on the same line as VINT) was measured in 2026-08
    // and found not to hold. The bound stays for a different reason: the frame-rewind
    // interlock in Raster_HInt's `.priming` arm reads any fire at V >= RASTER_VBLANK_V as
    // stale, so 223 is the last line a run may end on without authoring work into that region.
    // Literal, not a named constant, for the comptime-emission-scope reason given there.
    ensure(top + lines <= 223,
           "raster_ramp_program: run {top}..{top + lines - 1} must END at screen line 222 or above. The last line's LEAVE schedule puts a trailing fire one line below it, and the frame-rewind interlock (Raster_HInt's `.priming` arm) retires as stale any fire it reaches with the V counter at or past RASTER_VBLANK_V — so a run ending at 223 would be authoring work into the region the interlock treats as post-rewind. See the note at raster_gradient_program's matching ensure.")
    let addr = vdp_comm_addr(cmd)
    // Exactly one of the two supported targets, and the mask follows from which. INT
    // flags, not booleans, and the mask is built in a comptime var rather than an
    // if-expression: `if` is a statement here, and a value-position `if` in a struct
    // literal resolves as a LABEL rather than failing loudly (the silent-degradation
    // trap this module's opening note warns about — hit while writing this function).
    comptime var is_cram  = 0
    comptime var is_vsram = 0
    if cmd == vdp_comm(addr, VdpTarget.Cram,  VdpOp.Write) { is_cram  = 1 }
    if cmd == vdp_comm(addr, VdpTarget.Vsram, VdpOp.Write) { is_vsram = 1 }
    ensure(is_cram + is_vsram == 1,
           "raster_ramp_program: cmd {cmd} is neither a CRAM nor a VSRAM write command. A ramp re-issues this command every line and derives its pal_dirty_mask from the target, so an unrecognised one would both write the wrong place and re-assert a palette line computed from a non-palette address.")
    ensure(is_vsram == 0 || addr <= 78,
           "raster_ramp_program: VSRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..78")
    ensure(is_cram == 0 || addr <= 126,
           "raster_ramp_program: CRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..126")
    ensure(is_cram == 0 || (addr >> 5) != 0,
           "raster_ramp_program: the run writes CRAM line 0, the CHARACTER's palette line — a dense run re-asserts its line every frame, so this would fight the player's palette for the whole section")
    comptime var mask = 0
    if is_cram == 1 { mask = 1 << (addr >> 5) }
    return RasterRampProgram{
        rrp_mask:       mask,
        rrp_arm0:       raster_arm(1, top - 1),
        rrp_ops0:       0,
        rrp_arm1:       raster_arm(top - 1, top),       // == RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE
        rrp_ops1:       0,
        rrp_arm2:       RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE,
        rrp_ops2:       1,
        rrp_op:         OP_RUN_RAMP,
        rrp_cmd:        cmd,
        rrp_lines:      lines,
        rrp_start:      start,
        rrp_step:       step,
        rrp_end_arm:    RASTER_ARM_PARK,
        rrp_end_ops:    RASTER_OPS_END,
    }
}

// fp16 — build a 16.16 fixed-point value from whole and 1/256 parts, so a ramp's rate is
// authored as "pixels per line" rather than as a raw longword nobody can read.
//   fp16(1, 0)   = +1 px per line          fp16(0, 128) = +0.5 px per line
//   fp16(-1, 0)  = -1 px per line
pub comptime fn fp16(whole: int, frac256: int) -> int {
    ensure(frac256 >= 0 && frac256 <= 255, "fp16: frac256 {frac256} outside 0..255")
    ensure(whole >= -512 && whole <= 511, "fp16: whole {whole} outside -512..511")
    if whole < 0 { return (whole * 65536) - (frac256 * 256) }
    return (whole * 65536) + (frac256 * 256)
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_Install — stage a compiled program; it takes effect at the next
// Raster_VBlank, so an install can never tear a frame mid-walk.
//   a0 = program. NOT 0 — Raster_VBlank reads a pending 0 as "nothing staged this frame",
//   i.e. KEEP whatever is live, so a 0 here silently leaves the previous section's effect
//   running. `preset.emp` refuses it at the call site by redirecting to Raster_Program_None.
//   To turn the raster tier OFF, install an EMPTY program (Raster_Program_None): Raster_VBlank
//   recognises one and uninstalls the handler outright. The "0 = clear/uninstall" convention
//   this comment used to claim never worked — see EFX-7 and the note in Raster_VBlank.
// Main-loop context only.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_Install (a0: u32) clobbers() {
        move.l  a0, Raster_Pending
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_VBlank — once per frame from VInt_Level AND VInt_Lag.
//
// MUST run BEFORE Flush_VDP_Shadow: HBlank_Install arms reg $0A through the VDP
// shadow, so the arm only reaches the hardware on THIS frame's flush
// (unconditional, every register, every frame — see engine/system/vdp_init.emp)
// — a post-flush position would delay every arm a frame (Ruling 1d).
// It also ORs the program's pal_dirty_mask into Palette_Dirty, which must land
// before Enqueue_Dirty_Buffers reads the mask — Flush precedes Enqueue, so a
// pre-Flush position satisfies both orderings.
//
// Consumes a pending install, re-asserts the program's palette lines, rewinds the
// cursor to the first (priming) record, and leaves reg $0A = 0 so the pipeline
// primes on lines 0 and 1. Frame-top REGISTER state is not its job — the
// unconditional Flush_VDP_Shadow immediately after does that for all 19 registers.
//
// THE FRAME REWIND IS INTERLOCKED AGAINST A STALE FIRE, and the interlock lives in
// Raster_HInt's `.priming` arm rather than here — docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md's raster substrate
// item 3. Read that arm for the mechanism; what matters at this end is that the rewind below
// is NOT the only thing that has to be true at frame top: a fire deferred past this proc
// would consume priming record 0 and re-arm reg $0A behind the rewind's back, and `.priming`
// is what undoes it.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_VBlank () clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a2) {
        move.l  Raster_Pending, d0
        beq.s   .no_install
        clr.l   Raster_Pending
        // AN EMPTY PROGRAM UNINSTALLS THE HANDLER — it does not arm one that parks.
        //
        // This is EFX-7's close, and it closes it by making the teardown arm REACHABLE rather
        // than by deleting it. The arm used to be entered on `Raster_Pending == 0`, which the
        // `beq.s` two lines up had already filtered as "nothing pending": the documented
        // "0 = clear" convention could never fire, HBlank_Uninstall had no live caller, and IE1
        // was never dropped for the life of the ROM.
        //
        // The test is SEMANTIC rather than a sentinel value, and that is what makes it live
        // without a dormant scaffold. A program's second and third words are its first record's
        // arm word and op_count; a program whose FIRST record is already the terminator has no
        // records at all, which is exactly what Raster_Program_None is
        // ([0, RASTER_ARM_PARK, RASTER_OPS_END]) and what every "raster OFF" preset installs
        // today. So no authoring surface changes, no magic pending value exists to get wrong,
        // and any future empty program inherits the same treatment for the same reason.
        //
        // WHAT IT BUYS, MEASURED (oracle, 2026-08-16, before/after on the same scene): armed,
        // Raster_Program_None cost 512 cycles per frame across TWO HInt entries, forever, to
        // accomplish nothing. Two, not one, because `.park` does not advance the cursor: the
        // line-0 fire reads the terminator and parks, the counter still holds VBlank's 0 so
        // line 1 re-reads the SAME record, and only then does the $8AFF reload push the next
        // fire past the frame. After this change the handler is not entered at all — the
        // profiler has no row for it — and Raster_Program = 0 short-circuits the rest of this
        // proc every frame as well.
        //
        // Observationally equivalent otherwise: the program has no ops to run, and its
        // pal_dirty_mask is 0, so the frame-top re-assert this path skips had nothing to assert.
        movea.l d0, a1
        cmpi.w  #RASTER_OPS_END, 4(a1)          // word 2 = first record's op_count
        bne.s   .copy_program
        clr.l   Raster_Program
        // The patch table dies WITH the program. Raster_BuildSchedule's only liveness test is
        // Raster_Patch_Tab != 0 (Active_Buf is untouched on this path — that is the whole
        // reason the test is the table), so leaving it set here would keep the patcher
        // writing arm bytes into a buffer whose program has been torn down.
        clr.l   Raster_Patch_Tab
        // The ship entry dies with the program that declared it, exactly as the patch table does.
        // Enqueue_Dirty_Buffers tests only this pointer, so a stale one keeps queueing a dead
        // program's trailer as a frame-top DMA whenever the (also stale) anchor reads L <= 0.
        clr.l   Effects_Offscreen_Entry
        // TAIL CALL (§10). Nothing follows the uninstall on this arm, no stack frame exists
        // to unwind, and the `rts` this replaces was reached by fall-through only — no label,
        // so no branch is orphaned. HBlank_Uninstall clobbers(d0), inside this proc's
        // clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a2), so the caller's contract already covers it and the callee's
        // own `rts` returns straight to VInt_Level / VInt_Lag.
        jbra    HBlank_Uninstall
    .copy_program:
        move.l  d0, Raster_Program
        // A static ROM program is landing in Buf_A. A patch table left over from the outgoing
        // patched program describes a DIFFERENT program's records, so the builder would keep
        // re-recording that one over this buffer every VBlank and swap it live — the static
        // program would never be walked at all.
        clr.l   Raster_Patch_Tab
        // The ship entry dies with the program that declared it, exactly as the patch table does.
        // Enqueue_Dirty_Buffers tests only this pointer, so a stale one keeps queueing a dead
        // program's trailer as a frame-top DMA whenever the (also stale) anchor reads L <= 0.
        clr.l   Effects_Offscreen_Entry
        lea     Raster_Buf_A, a2
        move.l  a2, Raster_Active_Buf
        move.w  #(RASTER_BUF_SIZE / 2) - 1, d1
    .copy:
        move.w  (a1)+, (a2)+
        dbf     d1, .copy

    .no_install:
        // RE-RECORD the whole schedule from the ROM template into the INACTIVE buffer and
        // swap, BEFORE the cursor is rewound to the priming record — which then picks up the
        // buffer the swap just made live. This is the only place it may run: the arm words are
        // RELATIVE gaps, so a half-built schedule desynchronises the whole tail of the chain —
        // from the main loop it would race Raster_HInt walking a buffer during active display,
        // every frame the camera moves. Early-outs on Raster_Patch_Tab == 0, so a section with
        // no patched effect pays one move.l/beq.
        jbsr    Raster_BuildSchedule
        move.l  Raster_Program, d0
        beq.s   .done                   // no program armed: one tst per frame
        movea.l Raster_Active_Buf, a1
        // --- header ---
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // pal_dirty_mask
        or.b    d1, Palette_Dirty       // re-assert the base palette every frame
        // The whole header. There is no frame-top register replay here any more:
        // Flush_VDP_Shadow runs right after this and re-blits every shadowed VDP
        // register unconditionally, which restores anything a mid-frame op changed.
        // THE DENSE MODE IS FRAME STATE AND IS REWOUND WITH THE CURSOR. A dense run is
        // authored to finish inside its frame, but nothing enforces that: the mode is set only
        // by the ENTER ops and cleared only when .dense_body's countdown expires, so a run whose
        // line count reaches past line 223 never retires. Left set, the NEXT frame's very first
        // HInt — the priming fire on line 0 — takes .dense_body instead of the record walk and
        // streams from the previous frame's stale Raster_Dense_Cursor straight across the top of
        // the screen, re-arming reg $0A = 0 every line so the whole sparse schedule is dead too.
        // Clearing it here is the same act as rewinding the cursor below: both put the handler
        // back at frame top, and a run that ENTERs this frame re-seeds cursor, command and count
        // from its own op words anyway, so nothing legitimate is lost.
        //
        // IT IS THE MODE WORD AND NOT THE COUNTER (Tier-3 item 3): the counter is no longer the
        // run-active flag, so clearing it would leave a run armed with a stale zero count — which
        // .dense_body would then decrement to -1 and never retire, the exact stuck state this
        // rewind exists to prevent, in a new place.
        clr.w   Raster_Dense_Mode
        move.l  a1, Raster_Cursor       // first (priming) fire record
        // reg $0A = 0 primes the pipeline: no-op fires on lines 0 and 1 load the
        // first two real arm words. Re-installed every frame on purpose — it is
        // idempotent and it heals the counter the last record parked at $8AFF.
        moveq   #0, d0
        // PC-relative, not absolute: HBlank_Install stores this into the RAM jmp slot,
        // so a0 must end up holding Raster_HInt's true runtime address — which
        // `lea (pc)` yields just as well as an absolute operand, while keeping this
        // module's emitted bytes PLACEMENT-INVARIANT. That invariance is a property
        // the port harness probes (hblank_negative_probes' wrong-base probe asserts
        // the same section linked at two bases carries identical bytes), so an
        // absolute self-reference here would break a test for no gain.
        lea     Raster_HInt(pc), a0
        // TAIL CALL (§10). HBlank_Install clobbers(d1), inside clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a2); no stack
        // frame, and nothing runs after the install on this arm. The `rts` below is KEPT — it
        // is `.done`, the target of the `beq.s` on the no-program arm above, so folding it into
        // the branch would orphan that path. Zero bytes move here as a result: `bsr.w` becomes
        // `bra.w`, same width, and the retained `rts` is the one the other arm still needs.
        jbra    HBlank_Install
    .done:
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_HInt — the IRQ4 handler, entered by `jmp` from HBlank_Vector_Slot.
//
// Interrupt-transparent per the HBlankHandler contract: observable clobbers are
// EMPTY, the movem round trip is declared by preserves(), and it ends in `rte`.
//
// Per fire: publish this record's build-time arm word (FIRST — it is the one write
// that must not be lost, and it schedules gap(i+1 -> i+2) per Ruling 1b), then run
// this record's ops, then advance the cursor. No line compares, no delta math.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_HInt () clobbers() preserves(d0-d1/a1-a2) {
        // Raise to IPL 7 for the body. The 68000 enters IRQ4 at IPL 4, so IRQ6
        // (VBlank) can nest inside this handler — and a nested VBlank between an
        // OP_CRAM command longword and the colour words that follow it would
        // retarget the VDP address latch, sending our colours wherever VBlank left
        // it. Precedent: every long HInt payload in TF4 / Vectorman / Gunstar /
        // Alien Soldier opens by raising the mask (survey Q4).
        //
        // The engine's `ints_off_until_rte` bracket rather than a bare
        // `move.w #$2700,sr`: bare, the write needs `sr` in the contract, and
        // adding it to preserves() defeats the movem round-trip proof (the checker
        // cannot model `rte` restoring SR, so the whole clause stops verifying and
        // every saved register reports undeclared). The bracket declares its own
        // effect.
        //
        // THE RTE FLAVOUR, AND THE 30 CYCLES IT TAKES OFF EVERY FIRE (Tier-3 item 6,
        // 2026-08-19). This used to be the ordinary `ints_off`, whose acquire pushes
        // SR and whose release pops it back. In a handler both halves are dead: the
        // CPU already pushed the pre-interrupt SR onto the exception frame when it
        // vectored here, and the `rte` below reloads the whole register from that
        // frame — so the popped value (IPL 4, the level IRQ4 enters at) survives for
        // exactly one instruction before `rte` overwrites it. `move.w sr,-(sp)` is
        // 14 cycles in the PROLOGUE and `move.w (sp)+,sr` 16 in the epilogue; the
        // pair is pure overhead on every sparse fire AND on every dense scanline.
        //
        // The prologue half is the one with a second-order effect, and it is the
        // reason this could not be a quiet edit: dropping it moves the OP-WALK
        // ORIGIN 14 cycles earlier relative to the beam, so RASTER_HBLANK_END_CYC
        // — the distance from that origin to the line-start sampling instant — grows
        // by 14 and every solved blanking spin in raster_dsl.emp is re-derived
        // against it. Both constants were re-measured, not adjusted: see their notes.
        //
        // It also CLOSES a window rather than opening one. With the pop in place the
        // handler spent one instruction back at IPL 4 with the exception frame still
        // on the stack, where a level-6 VBlank could nest into a handler that had
        // already finished. Now the mask stays at 7 until `rte` drops it and returns
        // in the same instruction.
        //
        // `rte` MUST sit outside the bracket, and immediately after it — sigil
        // enforces exactly that (`[context.rte-undischarged]` fires on any path out
        // of the region that does not land on an `rte`, including an instruction
        // wedged between the closing brace and the return). Nothing else is legal
        // here, which is what makes "the release is the return" a proof rather than
        // a comment.
        with ints_off_until_rte {
        movem.l d0-d1/a1-a2, -(sp)
        lea     VDP_CTRL, a2
        // -------------------------------------------------------------------
        // DENSE-RUN FAST PATH (Task 5). While a gradient run is active the handler
        // bypasses the record walk: it fires every line, writes the constant CRAM
        // command + 3 stream colours, advances the cursor, and re-arms reg $0A=0.
        //
        // THE MODE-SWITCH SCHEDULE (both transitions, per Ruling 1b — the one-interrupt
        // arm lag). reg $0A is a CONSTANT 0 across the whole run, so there is no per-line
        // delta to pipeline; the lag only bites at the two edges:
        //   ENTER: the fire record carrying OP_RUN_GRADIENT is authored with arm word 0
        //     (raster_arm gap 1). That 0, written at the setup fire, is consumed at the
        //     NEXT fire's reload -> the first dense line fires one line after setup.
        //
        //     CORRECTED 2026-08-14. This comment used to conclude "author the fire at
        //     gradient_top - 2", which is the NAIVE derivation the hardware REJECTED:
        //     it authors the whole run one line high (with top=96 the seven ramp
        //     boundaries came out at 107/119/.../179 instead of 108/120/.../180). The
        //     measured setup line is T-1, not T-2 — see the T-1 note at the top of the
        //     dense-tier section, which records the measurement and why the dense path
        //     does not inherit the sparse -1. raster_gradient_program has always used
        //     T-1 (`rgp_arm1: raster_arm(top - 1, top)`); only this comment was stale,
        //     and it sat in the module docs/EFFECTS_AUTHORING.md names as the runtime
        //     source of truth. Anyone hand-authoring a dense program from here would
        //     have reproduced the exact bug the P2 gate already caught once.
        //   LEAVE: the SECOND-TO-LAST dense fire wrote reg $0A=0 and that arm is still in
        //     flight when the run ends, so ONE extra every-line fire happens after it, at
        //     the line below the run's last. It finds Mode==0, falls through to the record
        //     walk at Raster_Cursor (already past the gradient op), and writes the NEXT
        //     record's arm word. Because of the lag that arm governs the fire after next,
        //     so the first post-gradient sparse event must be scheduled >= 2 lines below
        //     the run's last line. The comptime authoring helper owns that gap; the run
        //     body does not.
        //
        //     THERE USED TO BE TWO, and the second is now suppressed at the LEAVE edge —
        //     the LAST dense fire parks the counter instead of re-arming it (`.dense_end`
        //     falls into `.park`; read that arm for why only ONE of the two is reachable
        //     from there and what it costs the format). The count went `lines + 5` ->
        //     `lines + 4`, measured on the FD1/FD2 pair and walked by
        //     tools/raster_frame_epoch_probe.py. The history is worth keeping because the
        //     derivation has been wrong in both directions here: the fixtures first
        //     predicted `lines + 4` when there really were two trailing fires and hardware
        //     answered `lines + 5`; the same number is now correct for a different reason.
        //     The >= 2 rule is untouched by any of it — the surviving trailing fire is the
        //     one that consumes the post-run record, exactly as before.
        // Conservative register model: the cursor is reloaded from Raster_Dense_Cursor
        // (RAM) every fire and only d0-d1/a1-a2 are saved. A reserved global stream
        // register (Gunstar's a6 / Alien Soldier's zero-save handler, survey Ruling 4c)
        // would drop the reload but changes engine-wide register conventions — a FLAGGED
        // decision needing user sign-off (see the report), not taken here.
        // -------------------------------------------------------------------
        tst.w   Raster_Dense_Mode
        bne     .dense_body
        movea.l Raster_Cursor, a1
        move.w  (a1)+, (a2)             // arm word -> reg $0A (consumed at fire i+1)
        move.w  (a1)+, d0               // op_count
        cmpi.w  #RASTER_OPS_END, d0
        beq     .park                   // terminator: stray fire, do not advance
        subq.w  #1, d0
        bmi     .priming                // priming / no-op record (unsized: OP_RUN_RAMP's
                                        // ENTER arm pushed this past a short displacement)
    .op_loop:
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // op — and this MOVE's own Z flag IS the OP_SET_REG test
        // Compare chain rather than a pc-indexed jump table: at this few opcodes it is
        // fewer cycles than the table setup, and it keeps the handler analyzable — a
        // computed `jmp` defeats the contract-closure dataflow, and
        // proc.clobber-undeclared is zero-firing by contract. The opcodes stay byte
        // offsets so a table can drop back in without touching the program format.
        //
        // OP_SET_REG IS DECIDED BEFORE THE CHAIN, AND FOR NO INSTRUCTION AT ALL (Tier-3
        // item 2). It is opcode ZERO and `move.w (a1)+, d1` sets Z from the word it just
        // moved, so the fetch above has ALREADY tested it: the `beq` below needs neither
        // a `tst.w` nor a `cmpi.w` ahead of it. That is the whole of the fix, and it is
        // why the deferred item's proposed `tst.w d1 / beq` is not what shipped — the
        // `tst.w` would have been 4 cycles buying a flag the fetch already set.
        //
        // WHAT IT COSTS AND WHAT IT BUYS, per class (raster_dsl.emp's dispatch model is
        // this paragraph in constants):
        //   OP_SET_REG   80 -> 10   the five failed rungs replaced by one TAKEN beq.s
        //   every other  +8         one NOT-TAKEN beq.s in front of its own rung
        // Register writes are the cheapest op in the vocabulary and were paying the most
        // to dispatch — 80 of an OP_SET_REG's 110 cycles were the compare chain it could
        // never match. They are also the op the S/H band shapes fire twice per band
        // (`reg_sh_on` at the top, `reg_sh_off` at the de-mix line), so this is the
        // dispatch cost the authoring vocabulary actually generates most of.
        //
        // WHY THE `beq` FORWARD AND NOT A `bne` AROUND AN INLINED BODY. The two spellings
        // trade the taken branch for the not-taken one: `beq .op_reg` costs SetReg 10
        // (taken) and everyone else 8 (not taken); `bne .dispatch` with the body inlined
        // here would cost SetReg 8 and everyone else 10. Shipped content decides it —
        // the three OJZ programs carry 2 reg_set against 3 stream ops, so the +8 side is
        // the one to make cheap. And the `beq` form keeps two properties the `bne` form
        // loses: OP_SET_REG stays the chain's FALL-THROUGH (an unrecognised opcode still
        // lands on the harmless one-word register write it has always landed on, rather
        // than on whichever body was promoted to fill the hole), and the whole change is
        // ONE inserted instruction — no code motion, so every rung's displacement, and
        // therefore every rung's .s encoding and its 16/18-cycle price, is untouched.
        beq     .op_reg
        cmpi.w  #OP_CRAM, d1
        beq.s   .op_cram
        cmpi.w  #OP_PAL_REGION, d1
        beq     .op_region
        cmpi.w  #OP_RUN_GRADIENT, d1
        beq     .op_run_gradient
        cmpi.w  #OP_RUN_RAMP, d1
        beq     .op_run_ramp
        cmpi.w  #OP_PAL_RESTORE, d1
        beq     .op_pal_restore
    .op_reg:
        // OP_SET_REG — one $8xxx word. Reached two ways, deliberately: by the free `beq`
        // at the top of the loop (opcode 0, the fast path), and by falling out of the
        // bottom of the chain (any opcode the chain does not recognise — unchanged
        // behaviour, and the reason this body stays HERE rather than moving up).
        move.w  (a1)+, (a2)
        dbf     d0, .op_loop
        jbra    .advance
    .op_cram:
        move.l  (a1)+, (a2)             // full CRAM command in ONE move.l (sets the address)
        // Burn a cycle-counted delay so the colour writes below land in HBlank (offscreen).
        // THE COUNT COMES FROM THE PROGRAM, not from a constant: what this delay has to
        // cover is the distance from THIS op's position in THIS fire to the blanking window,
        // and that distance depends on how many ops ran ahead of it and at what dispatch
        // depth. One global constant could only ever be right for one shape — see the
        // per-op solver in raster_dsl.emp. d1 is free here, before it is loaded with count.
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // spin iterations, comptime-solved for this op
    .cram_delay:
        dbf     d1, .cram_delay
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // count-1
        // -4(a2) IS VDP_DATA, and it is the CHEAPEST spelling of it available here
        // (substrate Tier-3 item 1). a2 holds VDP_CTRL = $C00004 across this whole arm —
        // the command longword above went through it — and VDP_DATA is $C00000, exactly
        // four bytes below, so the port is reachable as a 16-bit displacement off a
        // register we are already holding.
        //
        // 68000 MOVE.W timing, source (An)+ :  destination (xxx).L = 20 cycles
        //                                      destination d16(An) = 16 cycles
        // With the loop's own `dbf` (10 taken) that is 30 -> 26 cycles PER STREAMED WORD,
        // and 6 -> 4 bytes per instruction. It is also the burst's SPAN that shrinks: a
        // 3-word CRAM burst now spans 52 cycles from first write to last instead of 60,
        // which is budget handed straight back to the blanking window the solver aims at
        // (raster_dsl.emp, RASTER_STREAM_WORD_CRAM_CYC).
        //
        // WHY ONLY HERE. `.region_loop` and `.restore_loop` cannot have this: they have
        // repurposed a2 as their SOURCE cursor, so their destination has no base register
        // left to hang a displacement on and stays the 20-cycle absolute. Freeing one
        // costs more than it saves — measured on the cycle tables, an a1 save/restore
        // round trip is 20 cycles against a 4-cycle-per-word gain over at most 3 words —
        // which is why raster_dsl.emp prices the two classes SEPARATELY rather than
        // pretending one number covers both.
    .cram_loop:
        move.w  (a1)+, -4(a2)           // == VDP_DATA; 16 cyc, not the abs.L form's 20
        dbf     d1, .cram_loop
        dbf     d0, .op_loop
        jbra    .advance
    .op_region:
        // Stream count pre-derived colour words from a variant's Pal_Variant_Stage
        // bytes to CRAM. The program carries a comptime OFFSET into Pal_Variant_Stage
        // (slot*128 + line*32 + entry*2 — pal_stage_off below), NOT a link-time
        // pointer, so the whole program stays a flat [u16] of comptime literals. a2
        // (VDP_CTRL) is repurposed as the source cursor (colours go to VDP_DATA
        // absolute, which needs no a2), then restored for the arm write / next op — no
        // register is added to the movem save set (the ~60-cycle budget).
        move.l  (a1)+, (a2)             // CRAM write command longword (a2 = VDP_CTRL)
        // Same stream-from-program blanking delay as OP_CRAM (d1 free before count). The
        // region dispatches one rung DEEPER than cram, so it reaches here 16 cycles later
        // and needs a correspondingly smaller spin — which is exactly the kind of thing a
        // shared constant got wrong and the solver gets right.
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // spin iterations, comptime-solved for this op
    .region_delay:
        dbf     d1, .region_delay
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // count-1  (<= RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP)
        lea     Pal_Variant_Stage, a2   // staging base (was VDP_CTRL)
        adda.w  (a1)+, a2               // + comptime offset -> the derived colours
    .region_loop:
        move.w  (a2)+, VDP_DATA
        dbf     d1, .region_loop
        lea     VDP_CTRL, a2            // restore the control-port cursor
        dbf     d0, .op_loop
        jbra    .advance
    .op_run_gradient:
        // ENTER a dense run: stash the constant command, the line count, and the ROM
        // stream cursor into RAM, then fall through to .advance (Raster_Cursor now points
        // past the op, where the sparse walk resumes when the run ends). Every line of the
        // run is written by .dense_body on the subsequent every-line fires. Must be the
        // LAST op in its record (the record's arm word is authored 0 so the pipeline
        // starts firing every line — see the ENTER schedule at the handler top).
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Cmd     // constant CRAM write command
        move.w  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Lines   // L lines
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Cursor  // ROM stream pointer (L*3 words)
        // LAST, because it is the arming write: the mode word is what the next fire tests,
        // so the rest of the run's state must already be in place. +1 = stream the ROM
        // gradient (the sign is the body selector — see .dense_body).
        move.w  #1, Raster_Dense_Mode
        // fall through to .advance
        jbra    .advance
    .op_run_ramp:
        // ENTER a dense RAMP run. Same shape as the gradient ENTER — stash the constant
        // command and the line count, fall through to .advance — but seed the accumulator
        // from the program instead of a ROM cursor. The seed is deliberate: re-entering a
        // section restarts the ramp from its authored start rather than wherever the last
        // run left the accumulator, so the effect is not history-dependent.
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Cmd     // constant VDP write command (VSRAM or CRAM)
        move.w  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Lines   // L lines
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Ramp_Acc      // 16.16 start
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Ramp_Step     // 16.16 per-line delta (signed)
        move.w  #-1, Raster_Dense_Mode      // NEGATIVE = compute; the arming write, last
        // fall through to .advance
    .advance:
        move.l  a1, Raster_Cursor
        jbra    .out
    .priming:
        // THE FRAME-REWIND INTERLOCK (docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md, raster substrate item 3,
        // closed 2026-08-19). A no-op record is about to advance the cursor; first decide
        // whether this fire belongs to the frame at all.
        //
        // THE DEFECT. Raster_VBlank rewinds unconditionally — dense mode cleared, cursor back
        // on priming record 0, reg $0A re-armed to 0 — and until this arm existed the handler
        // carried no frame or line test whatsoever. A fire that runs AFTER the rewind but
        // BEFORE line 0 therefore walks priming record 0, writes ITS arm word over the 0 the
        // rewind just flushed, and advances the cursor. The next frame starts one record on
        // with the counter holding a mid-frame gap, and since the rewind repeats every frame,
        // so does the damage: fires are lost and the schedule never re-synchronises.
        //
        // HOW A FIRE GETS DEFERRED PAST A REWIND, MEASURED (tools/raster_frame_epoch_probe.py,
        // 2026-08-19) — and the measurement CORRECTS THE BOOKING, which blamed line 223. Fires
        // authored at 222, at 223 and even at 224 all retire before Raster_VBlank, every frame,
        // with an identical [2,6,10] cursor walk: the VDP raises the last active line's HINT far
        // enough ahead of VINT that the 68000 takes IRQ4 first. `top + lines <= 223` was
        // therefore never protecting anything. The real precondition is the CPU sitting at
        // IPL >= 4 across the VINT instant with an IRQ4 already raised — IRQ6 then wins the
        // arbitration and the IRQ4 is serviced after the rewind. The probe forces exactly that
        // with a long in-handler blanking spin (fire 222 spinning ~4,000 cycles, fire 224 raised
        // inside that window) and reproduces the shift: frames alternate [2,6,10] and [2,6], a
        // fire lost every other frame. Real code reaches the same state through any `ints_off`
        // bracket that straddles the end of active display — the VDP-access invariant at the top
        // of this file mandates such brackets around every main-loop command pair.
        //
        // WHY THE TEST IS A VDP READ AND NOT THE BOOKED ONE-BYTE FLAG. No software flag can tell
        // the two fires apart. A stale fire and the legitimate line-0 fire see BYTE-IDENTICAL
        // engine state — same cursor, same counter, same everything the rewind just wrote — and
        // any flag that self-clears on the first fire consumes the line-0 fire in the healthy
        // case and shifts the whole schedule down a line. The only thing that separates them is
        // WHERE THE BEAM IS, so the discriminator has to be the beam. (An epoch cleared by the
        // main loop was considered and rejected: on a lag frame the main loop does not reach the
        // clear before line 0, which turns the guard into a whole frame of lost effect.)
        //
        // WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN THE PROLOGUE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT. In the prologue this
        // costs every fire ~28 cycles AND moves the op-walk origin, which is the origin every
        // blanking spin is solved against — re-deriving RASTER_FIRE_BASE_CYC, HBLANK_END_CYC and
        // the spin word of every stream op in every program image. On this arm it costs the two
        // priming fires and NOTHING ELSE: a record with ops never reaches it, so no op-carrying
        // fire gains a cycle, the op-walk origin does not move, and not one solved spin changes.
        // And it is sufficient, because a stale fire ALWAYS lands on priming record 0 — that is
        // where the rewind put the cursor, and the rewind is the thing being interlocked.
        //
        // IT ALSO CANNOT RETIRE REAL WORK, and that is a property of the AUTHORING surface
        // rather than a hope: `raster_dsl.fire` refuses a fire with no ops, so the only zero-op
        // records any program can contain are the two priming records — and the priming pair
        // fires on lines 0 and 1 by construction, because Raster_VBlank leaves reg $0A = 0.
        // A record with ops cannot reach this arm, and no in-frame record without ops can exist
        // above line 1. The one remaining way to author a fire into V >= RASTER_VBLANK_V is a
        // dense run ending at 223, which both constructors refuse — the `ensure` beside
        // RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE is what ties that bound to this threshold.
        //
        // THE INSERTION DID NOT MOVE THE DISPATCH CHAIN'S PRICES, and that was measured rather
        // than assumed: this arm sits below `.advance`, so every rung's target shifted 20 bytes
        // and the deepest one (`.op_pal_restore`, whose header warns that a mid-chain insertion
        // risks a silent word-relaxation) could have gained cycles. F8's marginal per-fire cost
        // came out identical either side — 676.0 — as did F3, F4 and F5. No rung relaxed.
        //
        // $C00008 IS THE HV COUNTER, reachable as a displacement off the VDP_CTRL a2 already
        // holds (the same trick `.cram_loop`'s `-4(a2)` uses for VDP_DATA). Its high byte is the
        // V counter, so `>= $E000` is "V >= 224", i.e. outside active display — true for the
        // whole of VBlank including the NTSC counter's jump back to $E5, and false for every
        // line 0..223 a priming fire can legitimately land on. Deliberately NOT the status
        // register's VBlank bit: reading VDP_CTRL resets the command-latch write-pending state
        // and clears the status F flag, while the HV counter read has no side effects at all
        // (reg $00 bit 1 is 0 — boot_data.emp: "HV counter readable"), and status bit 3 also
        // reads set whenever the display is off, which would silently retire every fire during
        // a fade.
        //
        // THE REPAIR IS "UNDO", NOT "PARK". Writing RASTER_ARM_PARK here would leave reg $0A at
        // 255 and kill the next frame outright — the opposite of the fix. What the rewind wants
        // is reg $0A = 0 and the cursor still on record 0, so the stale fire restores exactly
        // that: re-write the every-line word this record's arm just clobbered, and return
        // WITHOUT advancing. The counter is reloaded from reg $0A on every line outside active
        // display, so even if a reload caught the clobbered value mid-repair, the last reload
        // before line 0 sees the restored 0.
        move.w  4(a2), d1                       // $C00008 — HV counter, V in the high byte
        cmpi.w  #(RASTER_VBLANK_V << 8), d1     // V >= 224: outside active display
        bcs     .advance                        // in-frame fire: the normal priming walk
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE, (a2)    // undo this record's arm write (== reg $0A = 0)
        jbra    .out                            // ... and do NOT advance the cursor
    .op_pal_restore:
        // Band OFF edge: stream base colours from the ship snapshot. .op_region's shape
        // with TWO deliberate differences: the source is Palette_Ship_Snap (whose offset
        // IS the CRAM byte address — BuildStaticDMA maps Palette_Buffer+$00/20/40/60 to
        // CRAM $00/20/40/60 1:1), and its delay spin — like every stream op's since
        // substrate item 1c — is SOLVED for this op at this position, from a blanking
        // window measured on hardware. There is no per-class knob here any more and no
        // ladder to walk: the restore's depth-4 dispatch (82 cyc) and its deeper
        // pre-burst arm (56 cyc against cram's 36) are inputs the solver reads, which is
        // what the old "its OWN knob, never EFX_BLANK_DELAY" was groping at.
        //
        // THE HISTORY, KEPT BECAUSE IT IS EVIDENCE AND NOT MERELY PROVENANCE. The R1
        // §7.3 first-datum capture (2026-08-17) put the burst at ZERO delay at x~180 of
        // the row ABOVE the authored edge, and the bare single-op cram ON fire spilled
        // identically at x~170 — the finding that the clean mixed-shape evidence never
        // covered single-op fires at all. The value jumped to from that deficit, 13, was
        // pixel-verified (authored edge row fully tinted, next row fully base) and is now
        // REPRODUCED by the solver as the latest spin whose 3-word burst still beats the
        // sampling instant (raster_dsl.emp, PIN 3). The solver ships 10 instead: 13 sat
        // at the window's far edge, and centring gives 30 cycles back.
        //
        // The 110-cycle single-op-vs-two-op gap that framed all of this is still exactly
        // right and is now spent rather than argued about: an OP_SET_REG costs
        // RASTER_OP_FETCH_CYC 8 + RASTER_DISPATCH_RUNG_CYC 16 * RASTER_DISPATCH_RUNGS 5 +
        // RASTER_WORK_REG_CYC 12 + RASTER_OP_TAIL_CYC 10 = 110, which is fixture F1's pin
        // (412 = base 302 + 110), and it is exactly the 11 iterations by which a solved
        // second-position spin undercuts the same op's leading spin.
        move.l  (a1)+, (a2)             // CRAM write command longword (a2 = VDP_CTRL)
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // spin iterations, comptime-solved for this op
    .restore_delay:
        dbf     d1, .restore_delay
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // count-1  (<= RASTER_BURST_MAX_DEEP — also bounds the (a2)+ reads inside the 128-byte snapshot)
        lea     Palette_Ship_Snap, a2   // snapshot base (was VDP_CTRL)
        // + offset == CRAM byte address (payload D-F; even + in-line by the pal_restore
        // constructor's ensures — an odd word here would address-error the (a2)+ reads)
        adda.w  (a1)+, a2
    .restore_loop:
        move.w  (a2)+, VDP_DATA
        dbf     d1, .restore_loop
        lea     VDP_CTRL, a2            // restore the control-port cursor
        dbf     d0, .op_loop
        jbra    .advance
    .dense_body:
        // One dense line: keep firing every line, write the constant command + this kind's
        // per-line payload, count down. When Raster_Dense_Lines reaches 0 the body clears
        // Raster_Dense_Mode, so the NEXT fire finds it clear and resumes the sparse record
        // walk at Raster_Cursor (LEAVE schedule at the handler top). Conservative: cursor
        // reloaded from RAM, no reserved register.
        //
        // WHICH BODY, DECIDED FOR ONE BRANCH AND NO LOAD (Tier-3 item 3). The kind cannot
        // change inside a run, yet this used to re-answer it every scanline with a second
        // RAM read (`tst.w Raster_Dense_Kind`) plus its own branch, on top of the run-active
        // test the handler had ALREADY done at the top. Raster_Dense_Mode carries both facts
        // in one signed word — 0 no run, +1 gradient, -1 ramp — so the `tst.w` above answers
        // "is a run active" for the `bne`, and its N flag answers "which body" for the `bmi`
        // here. The whole hoist is that this `bmi.s` replaces a load-and-branch pair.
        //
        // WHAT IT IS ACTUALLY WORTH, MEASURED — and it is NOT what the deferred item booked.
        // The item said ~2,300 cyc/frame, priced as 24 cycles per line at 96 lines. On
        // hardware the whole hoist is **4 cycles per line**: 354.0 -> 350.0 on the FD1/FD2
        // fixture pair (tools/raster_cost_probe.py; RASTER_DENSE_LINE_GRAD_CYC).
        //
        // THE MISSING EIGHT CYCLES ARE THE VDP, and this is the finding worth keeping. The
        // removed pair is `tst.w (xxx).W` (12) plus a not-taken `bcc.s` (8) against the
        // `bmi.s` (8) that replaces them — an instruction model says 12. But the test sat
        // directly after `move.l Raster_Dense_Cmd, (a2)`, a control-port write, and during
        // active display the VDP holds the bus; the EA read the `tst.w` would have paid for
        // is absorbed into a wait the CPU was already serving. Confirmed independently, in
        // the other direction, by the parcel's cost-gate poison: inserting a
        // behaviour-neutral `tst.w` in that same position measured +4, not +12. Nominal
        // 68000 timings over-predict any edit inside this body, and the fixture pair is now
        // the only thing that can tell you by how much.
        //
        // AND IT CARRIES A FIXED COST: +34 cycles per RUN per frame (the ENTER's
        // `move.w #1` over the old `clr.w`, and the `.dense_end` retire below), measured as
        // the FD1/FD2 intercept. So the crossover is around NINE lines — a dense run shorter
        // than that is very slightly slower than before. Nothing shipped is near it (both
        // OJZ runs are 96 lines, where the net is -350 cyc/frame), and a run of eight lines
        // is a sparse-tier job anyway, but it is a real property and it is stated rather
        // than rounded away.
        //
        // WHY NOT A RAM-HELD BODY POINTER, which is what the deferred item proposed. It
        // costs MORE even on paper: sigil places this RAM in the absolute-SHORT window, so
        // `movea.l (xxx).W, a1` is 16 cycles and `jmp (a1)` 8, i.e. 24 against the 20 the
        // two-instruction test cost — a net loss before considering that a computed `jmp`
        // defeats the contract-closure dataflow (the same objection that keeps the opcode
        // dispatch a compare chain, `.op_loop` above). One conditional branch is the floor
        // here, not a compromise: reaching two bodies from one taken `bne` cannot be done in
        // zero branches, and making the TOP a three-way instead would put a second
        // not-taken branch on the SPARSE path and move RASTER_FIRE_BASE_CYC — re-basing
        // every solved blanking spin and every fixture pin to buy 8 cycles on a path that
        // fires four times a frame, for a path that fires ninety-six.
        //
        // THE PROLOGUE IS DUPLICATED INTO BOTH BODIES, deliberately. It cannot be shared
        // above the branch: `move.w #imm, (a2)` and `move.l (xxx).L, (a2)` both set the
        // flags from the data they move, so the N flag the `tst.w` produced does not
        // survive them. Ten bytes, zero cycles.
        //
        // THE THREE STREAM WRITES ARE `-4(a2)`, the same spelling `.cram_loop` got in
        // substrate Tier-3 item 1 and the rider that item booked against this body. a2 is
        // VDP_CTRL ($C00004) here — `lea VDP_CTRL, a2` at the top of the handler, and
        // nothing on the dense path touches it; the two writes immediately above go
        // THROUGH it. VDP_DATA is $C00000, four bytes below, so the port is a `d16(An)`
        // displacement off a register already in hand: MOVE.W `(An)+` -> `d16(An)` is 16
        // cycles and 4 bytes against the absolute long form's 20 and 6.
        //
        // WHY THIS IS SAFE, and it is a statement about WHEN each write lands, not about
        // how many cycles the body costs. The destination extension words are prefetched
        // ahead of the write, so the cycles the short form stops paying land AFTER the
        // write they belong to, not before it: the FIRST write does not move, and writes
        // 2 and 3 move EARLIER by 4 and 8. All three therefore stay strictly inside
        // [first write, old third write] — the burst contracts from its own tail and
        // cannot leave the blanking window on either side. The dense body carries no
        // solved spin (unlike every sparse stream op), so there is no blanking
        // calibration to re-derive either.
        //
        // WHAT IT MEASURED, AND THE PREDICTION IT REFUTED. Nominal says 3 x 4 = 12 cycles
        // a line. The note above warns that nominal OVER-predicts edits in this body — the
        // hoist's nominal 12 measured 4, and the cost gate's poison inserted a nominally-12
        // instruction for +4 — so this edit was predicted at ~4 on that same ratio. It
        // measured the FULL 12: 328.0 -> 316.0 on the FD1/FD2 pair, intercept unchanged at
        // 1512. The two results are not in conflict once the absorbed thing is named. The
        // hoist removed a `tst.w (xxx).W`, an OPERAND read directly behind a control-port
        // write, where the CPU was already stalled waiting on the VDP and the read rode
        // that wait for free. This removes one INSTRUCTION-STREAM word per write, which the
        // 68000 fetches on its own account and no VDP wait state pays for. So the warning
        // stands but its scope is narrower than it read: the bus absorbs adjacent DATA
        // accesses, not code size. Nothing here was booked before it was measured, and the
        // FD pair remains the only thing that can settle either case
        // (RASTER_DENSE_LINE_GRAD_CYC).
        bmi.s   .ramp_body
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE, (a2)       // reg $0A=0: fire every line
        move.l  Raster_Dense_Cmd, (a2)             // constant command (sets the address)
        movea.l Raster_Dense_Cursor, a1
        move.w  (a1)+, -4(a2)                      // == VDP_DATA; 16 cyc, not abs.L's 20
        move.w  (a1)+, -4(a2)
        move.w  (a1)+, -4(a2)
        move.l  a1, Raster_Dense_Cursor
        // The countdown's own Z flag ends the run — the `bra .out` this replaces cost the
        // same 10 cycles taken, so retiring the mode word is free on every line but the last.
        subq.w  #1, Raster_Dense_Lines
        bne.s   .out
        jbra    .dense_end
    .ramp_body:
        // One ramp line: advance the 16.16 accumulator and emit its INTEGER half. `swap`
        // is the whole trick — it moves the high word down with no shift loop, so the
        // fixed-point divide is free. This is Ristar's per-line body (add, shift, store)
        // with the shift folded into the word select.
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE, (a2)       // reg $0A=0: fire every line
        move.l  Raster_Dense_Cmd, (a2)             // constant command (sets the address)
        move.l  Raster_Ramp_Acc, d1
        add.l   Raster_Ramp_Step, d1
        move.l  d1, Raster_Ramp_Acc
        swap    d1                                 // high word = integer part
        // `-4(a2)` == VDP_DATA, as in .dense_body above (a2 is still VDP_CTRL — the two
        // writes at the top of this body went through it). MOVE.W `Dn` -> `d16(An)` is 12
        // cycles and 4 bytes against the absolute long form's 16 and 6. There is only ONE
        // write here, so the safety statement is even simpler than the gradient body's:
        // the saved cycles are the destination extension word, prefetched ahead of the
        // write, so the write itself does not move at all — only the retirement after it.
        move.w  d1, -4(a2)                         // == VDP_DATA; 12 cyc, not abs.L's 16
        subq.w  #1, Raster_Dense_Lines
        bne.s   .out
    .dense_end:
        // The run is over. Nothing else clears this word, and until it is clear every fire
        // still lands here — so this is the LEAVE edge, and it is reached once per run.
        //
        // AND IT FALLS INTO `.park`, WHICH ENDS THE SCHEDULE (Tier-3 dense rider, the two
        // trailing fires). The body above has already written the every-line word to reg $0A
        // this line; parking overwrites it with $8AFF before the counter reloads, so the arm
        // this LAST dense fire leaves in flight schedules nothing. That kills the SECOND of
        // the two trailing fires — the one that walked the terminator a second time and
        // changed nothing. MEASURED at 232 cyc/frame per dense run on the FD1/FD2 pair
        // (tools/raster_cost_probe.py: FD1 4040 -> 3808, FD2 14152 -> 13920, the SAME delta
        // at both legs with the per-line slope bit-identical at 316.0, which is what a cost
        // charged once per run and to nothing else looks like).
        //
        // ONE, NOT TWO, AND THE ARITHMETIC IS RULING 1B'S. The rider was booked as "falling
        // `.dense_end` into `.park` would suppress both" at ~600 cyc/frame; the pipeline says
        // otherwise and the frame-epoch probe agrees. An arm written at fire i governs
        // gap(i+1 -> i+2), so the two trailing fires are consuming the arms of the last TWO
        // dense fires: trailing fire 1 consumes the SECOND-TO-LAST dense line's, trailing
        // fire 2 the last's. This edge can only reach the last one. Suppressing the first as
        // well means the second-to-last line must park too, which is a "one line remaining"
        // test inside the per-line body — the countdown's own flags answer "zero", not "one",
        // and the count lives in RAM. At the cheapest spelling's ~4 cyc/line that pays for
        // itself only below ~58 lines and both shipped runs are 96. The slope is not spent to
        // buy the intercept; see the closure block in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md.
        //
        // WHAT IT COSTS THE FORMAT, stated because it is a real narrowing and not a free
        // win. Before this, the sparse walk resumed after a run and could in principle carry
        // on indefinitely; now the schedule ENDS with the run, bar the one fire already in
        // flight. Nothing representable loses anything: the dense tier is authorable only
        // through `raster_gradient_program` / `raster_ramp_program`, both fixed structs whose
        // terminator follows the setup record immediately (raster_dsl has no dense op
        // constructor at all), so every dense program in the language walks the terminator
        // with these fires and nothing else. A future dense-then-sparse composition wants an
        // authored LEAVE arm word in the op body — the sparse tier's own answer, where the
        // last two records carry $8AFF from DATA — not this pipeline leak.
        //
        // IT ALSO PULLS THE LAST FIRE BACK INSIDE ACTIVE DISPLAY. A legal run may end at 222,
        // which used to put trailing fires on 223 AND 224 — the second one past
        // RASTER_VBLANK_V, benign only because it lands on `.park` rather than `.priming`.
        // After this there is exactly one, at 223, and no representable program can fire in
        // VBlank at all.
        clr.w   Raster_Dense_Mode
        // fall through
    .park:
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_PARK, (a2)
    .out:
        movem.l (sp)+, d0-d1/a1-a2
        }
        rte
}

// The canonical "explicitly no raster effects here" program: no palette
// re-assert and a terminator that parks the counter. Installing it is how a
// section turns a neighbour's effect OFF.
pub data Raster_Program_None: [u16; 3] = [0, RASTER_ARM_PARK, RASTER_OPS_END]

// ===========================================================================
// PATCHED TEMPLATES (effects P2 Task 7, generalised in P3 P-a/P-b) — a raster program
// whose boundary lines MOVE at runtime without rebuilding the program. The back buffer
// Raster_Buf_B (reserved by P1 for exactly this) holds a runtime COPY of a template;
// Raster_BuildSchedule RE-RECORDS it from the ROM template each VBlank — into whichever of
// Buf_A / Buf_B is not live, then swaps Raster_Active_Buf. A boundary move is a re-derived arm
// word; a boundary that has left its band is a record the builder simply does not emit.
//
// P-b DELETED THE SINGLE-CHANNEL WATER SLOT that started this. WATER_TEMPLATE_ARM0_OFF
// was a fixed byte offset 2 — the priming arm word of record 0 — which is where a
// one-boundary program keeps its only moving part. That constant is gone because it was
// the shape of the limit: one patch site per program, found by a magic offset rather
// than described. P-a's patch table carried a resolved arm_off PER RECORD and P-b's patcher
// walked them, so the number of moving boundaries became a property of the content rather
// than of the runtime. Local removal then deleted arm_off outright — the builder emits the
// records itself, so it knows where every arm word landed and needs nothing pre-resolved;
// the table carries rec_off/rec_len instead, describing where each record IS rather than
// which word to poke. (The offset-2 fact itself survives, unnamed, inside raster_dsl's
// arm_word_index: the header is ONE word, so record 0's arm is always word 1 —
// [pal_dirty_mask 0][arm0 2][opc0 4][arm1 6]... — which is what lets a rec_off be
// pre-resolved and what makes the builder's 5-word prologue a constant.)

// Raster_WaterArm — the priming arm word that lands the water fire at screen line M.
// FORMULA (survey Ruling 1b — the one-interrupt arm lag): priming fires 0 and 1 land on
// lines 0 and 1 (Raster_VBlank leaves reg $0A=0); the arm word WRITTEN at priming record
// 0 is consumed at the NEXT reload and schedules gap(1 -> event). The event fire must be
// at M-1 so its writes land on M (Ruling 1a). Priming fire 1 is at line 1, so the gap
// 1 -> (M-1) needs reg $0A = (M-1) - 1 - 1 = M - 3. So arm0 = $8A00 | (M - 3).
//
// RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE is the `- 3` above, named ONCE: engine.effects.raster_dsl's
// arm_at reduces to $8A00 | (M - 3) for a single-event program and pins itself against
// this constant; Raster_BuildSchedule's `subq.w #1, d2` screen-line -> fire-line step is the
// runtime twin. They were previously two hand-synced literals with only a comment
// holding them together.
pub const RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE = 3
pub const RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE = 223

// THE INTERLOCK'S THRESHOLD AND THE AUTHORING CEILING ARE ONE FACT, and this is where the two
// halves are tied together. Raster_HInt's `.priming` arm retires a fire as stale when the V
// counter reads >= RASTER_VBLANK_V, i.e. when the beam has left active display. That is only a
// safe test while no fire can be AUTHORED to land there: raise RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE to 224 and
// a legitimate last-line fire starts reading as stale, so the interlock would quietly retire
// real work instead of stale work — a guard that has begun eating the thing it guards.
//
// It is stated as an equation rather than an inequality on purpose. `<=` would also admit a
// ceiling far BELOW the threshold, which is not a drift worth allowing silently either: the
// two numbers describe the same screen edge from opposite sides, so they move together or the
// build stops. Nothing else in the tree relates them — raster_dsl pins its own inlined bounds
// against RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE and has never heard of the V counter.
ensure(RASTER_VBLANK_V == RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE + 1,
       "the frame-rewind interlock's V threshold ({RASTER_VBLANK_V}) and the authoring ceiling ({RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE}) have drifted apart. Raster_HInt's `.priming` arm retires any fire reaching it with V >= {RASTER_VBLANK_V} as a stale post-rewind fire; a fire authored on line {RASTER_VBLANK_V} or later would be retired as stale even though it is real work the author asked for. Move both or neither.")

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_InstallPatched — install a patched template.
//   a0 = patched template
//
// IT NO LONGER SEEDS THE ANCHORS (Parcel W0, 2026-08-15). The seed lived here while the
// patched program was the anchor bank's only reader, and that made Effects_World_Y the one
// channel a preset did NOT total-bind: a section without a patched program never wrote the
// bank and silently inherited the previous section's anchors — measured on the shipped ROM
// as 224/314 surviving the crossing from OJZ section 0 into section 1. Seeding now happens
// unconditionally in Effects_InstallPreset, which is what "TOTAL BINDING" already meant for
// every other channel (§7.12). The anchor bank has a second reader coming (Parcel W's
// parallax overlay) that is NOT gated on a patched program, so the old placement was not
// merely inconsistent — it was unsound for anything but the single reader it grew up with.
//
// ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, and it survived the copy's deletion with its reason intact but its
// mechanism changed. Raster_Patch_Tab is set FIRST, before the stores below point Active_Buf and
// Raster_Program at a buffer — because those two are what make the buffer look live to
// Raster_VBlank, and a Patch_Tab still holding the PREVIOUS template (or 0 at first install, which
// would read the vector table as a record count) inside that window is the race the builder's
// VBlank timing would otherwise expose. It used to say "before the COPY points Active_Buf at
// Buf_B"; there is no copy any more — the builder re-records from the ROM template every VBlank —
// but the window between "looks live" and "has a valid table" is the same window, and the table
// still has to win it.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_InstallPatched (a0: u32) clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a2) {
        lea     RASTER_BUF_SIZE(a0), a1
        move.l  a1, Raster_Patch_Tab            // table first

        // ---- the off-screen ship pointer, derived from the ROM template ----
        // Trailer layout: [n (word)] then, per shipping channel, [channel][stage offset]
        // [colour count][CRAM byte address][reg count][reg words...] — sitting after the patch
        // table at RASTER_BUF_SIZE + 2 + 10*records. Read the record count the table already
        // starts with and step over it.
        //
        // IT HOLDS PARAMETERS, NOT A BUILT DMAEntry. This line used to read
        // "[channel (word)][DMAEntry (14 bytes)]"; there is no DMAEntry anywhere in the ROM
        // trailer. Pal_Variant_Stage's address is a LINK-time value, and folding one into the
        // emitted program image makes that image non-comptime, which breaks the whole-image
        // `first_mismatch(patched_program(...), hand_twin)` pin every patched fixture rests on —
        // so Raster_BuildShipEntry (below) adds the base and builds the 14-byte entry into
        // Static_Pal_Ship at install time instead. Its own header, buffers.emp's replay site
        // ("Entry layout after the channel word: [stage off][colour count][CRAM addr][reg
        // count][reg words...]") and ship_trailer's emitter all described this correctly
        // already; only this line disagreed.
        //
        // TEN BYTES PER ENTRY, AND TEN IS NOT A SHIFT. The stride was a single `lsl.w #3`
        // while entries were four words; the fifth word (rec_off/rec_len replacing arm_off)
        // makes it 10n, and `mulu` is forbidden (CODING_CONVENTIONS.md). 8n + n + n is three
        // instructions and no multiplier.
        //
        // FROM THE ROM TEMPLATE, NEVER THE RAM WORKING COPY. The descriptor is fully static —
        // nothing in it is ever patched — and the runtime's template copy is a fixed 128 bytes
        // that stops before the table, so the trailer exists only here.
        //
        // Written on BOTH paths below, so no patched program can inherit the previous one's
        // entry. The preset's non-patched path clears it in Effects_InstallPreset.
        move.w  (a1), d0                        // record count
        move.w  d0, d1
        lsl.w   #3, d0                          // 8n
        add.w   d1, d0                          // 9n
        add.w   d1, d0                          // 10n — the table's byte length
        addq.w  #2, d0                          // past the count word itself
        // THE POINTER IS INERT FOR THE WHOLE INSTALL WINDOW, and that ordering is a fix rather
        // than tidiness. The pointer used to be published HERE, ~10 instructions before
        // Raster_BuildShipEntry below fills Static_Pal_Ship from it. Enqueue_Dirty_Buffers tests
        // only this pointer and then queues Static_Pal_Ship, so a VBlank landing in that window
        // shipped the PREVIOUS program's source/destination/length under this program's trailer —
        // wrong colours into CRAM, on a section crossing, on a frame the anchor happened to be
        // off-screen. Not a narrow race either: preset.emp's install note records that a VBlank
        // lands in this window ROUTINELY.
        //
        // Clearing first is what makes the window safe: 0 is the consumer's inert value (it is the
        // same value the .no_ship path and Raster_VBlank's teardown write), so a VBlank mid-install
        // ships nothing for one frame instead of shipping something wrong. The publish is a single
        // move.l, which no interrupt can split, so the transition needs no ints_off bracket.
        clr.l   Effects_Offscreen_Entry
        tst.w   (a1, d0.w)                      // the trailer's own count: 0 = no ship
        beq     .no_ship
        addq.w  #2, d0                          // past it, onto the channel word
        lea     (a1, d0.w), a2                  // held in a2 until the entry behind it exists
        jbra    .ship_done
    .no_ship:
        suba.l  a2, a2                          // a2 = 0: nothing to publish
    .ship_done:
        // A dense run in flight belongs to the OUTGOING program. Nothing else clears it, so
        // crossing from the gradient section into a patched one mid-run leaves the handler in
        // .dense_body streaming a stale cursor while this program's records are never walked.
        // The MODE word is the run-active flag (Tier-3 item 3), so it is what disarms a run.
        clr.w   Raster_Dense_Mode
        // The install half of what Raster_CopyPatchedTemplate used to do. The COPY is gone — the
        // builder re-records the whole schedule from the ROM template at the next VBlank — but
        // these three stores are not the copy and nothing else performs them. Buf_B is named here
        // only as a starting side; the builder swaps buffers from this frame on.
        lea     Raster_Buf_B, a1
        move.l  a1, Raster_Active_Buf
        move.l  a1, Raster_Program              // nonzero -> Raster_VBlank processes it
        clr.l   Raster_Pending                  // a staged static program loses (preset.emp's guard)
        // The ship entry is built AFTER the install stores, not beside the pointer computation
        // above. That used to be a register constraint (Raster_BuildShipEntry needs a0, and the
        // deleted copy loop was still running it as a template cursor until it returned); a0 is
        // untouched by this proc since the initial `lea` and free the whole way through now, so the
        // ordering is no longer forced — kept here to match the entry's installation site.
        //
        // a2 SURVIVES the call: Raster_BuildShipEntry declares clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a1), so the
        // trailer pointer computed above is still live for the publish below. That is what lets the
        // build precede the publish instead of the other way round.
        move.l  a2, d0
        beq     .no_build
        movea.l a2, a1
        jbsr    Raster_BuildShipEntry
    .no_build:
        // PUBLISHED LAST, once Static_Pal_Ship actually describes THIS program. On the .no_ship
        // path a2 is 0 and this is the clear the old code did up in the branch.
        move.l  a2, Effects_Offscreen_Entry
        // NO TAIL CALL INTO THE BUILDER. Raster_PatchAll used to be invoked from here, i.e. from
        // the MAIN LOOP, which was survivable while it wrote one byte per record and is not now:
        // the builder re-records the entire buffer, and Raster_HInt walks that buffer during
        // active display. The install lands at the next Raster_VBlank, which is the contract this
        // proc always had ("it takes effect at the next Raster_VBlank, so an install can never
        // tear a frame mid-walk"). This also removes a PRE-EXISTING hazard: the deleted copy blatted
        // 128 bytes over the live buffer from the main loop.
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_BuildShipEntry — lay the off-screen ship's DMAEntry from the trailer's parameters.
//   a1 = trailer entry: [channel][stage offset][colour count][CRAM byte address]
//
// THE TRAILER CARRIES PARAMETERS RATHER THAN A BUILT ENTRY, and the reason is a language
// constraint rather than a preference: Pal_Variant_Stage's address is a LINK-time value, and
// folding one into the emitted program image makes that image non-comptime — which breaks
// `first_mismatch(patched_program(...), hand_twin)`, the whole-image pin every patched
// fixture rests on. So the base is added HERE, where an address is just an address.
//
// It also means no second site knows the DMAEntry byte layout: this fills in the parameters
// and hands them to Build_DMA_Entry, the one builder.
//
// EVERY REGISTER IT TOUCHES IS INSIDE d0-d4/a0-a1, and that is a contract-closure constraint
// rather than tidiness. The first version used d5 for Build_DMA_Entry's marker longword and
// saved it around the call; the closure gate still failed, reporting `transitive d5` against
// Effects_InstallPreset, Parallax_CheckBoundary and Raster_InstallPatched — the analysis reads
// the DECLARATION, so restoring a register does not buy back its declaration, and declaring
// it propagates up the whole caller chain. Build_DMA_Entry's marker register moved to d4,
// which those callers already declare. a0 is free here because this runs AFTER the template
// copy, which is what needed it.
// -----------------------------------------------
proc Raster_BuildShipEntry (a1: u32) clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a1) {
        // source: Pal_Variant_Stage + the fire's own staging offset, as (address >> 1)
        lea     Pal_Variant_Stage, a0
        move.w  2(a1), d1
        adda.w  d1, a0                          // adda.w, NOT add.w: add.w dN,aM mis-encodes
        move.l  a0, d1
        lsr.l   #1, d1
        andi.l  #$7FFFFF, d1
        // length: colour count -> words, which is what reg $93/$94 counts
        move.w  4(a1), d3
        // destination: CRAM byte address -> the command longword, through the shared builder
        moveq   #0, d2
        move.w  6(a1), d2
        vdp_comm_reg(d2, VdpTarget.Cram, VdpOp.Dma)
        lea     Static_Pal_Ship, a0
        moveq   #-$6C, d0                       // $94 sign-extended
        move.l  #$93979695, d4                  // reg markers $93/$97/$96/$95
        jbra    Build_DMA_Entry
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_BuildSchedule — RE-RECORD the frame's schedule from the ROM template into the INACTIVE
// buffer, emitting only the records that are live this frame, then swap.
//
// It replaces Raster_PatchAll, which rewrote one arm BYTE per record in the live buffer. The
// patcher could move a boundary but could never REMOVE one: arm words are relative gaps and the
// handler's cursor advance is implicit, so a record is left behind only by having been walked.
// Emitting the schedule instead makes removal a matter of not copying it.
//
// THE ARM SLOT IS TWO RECORDS BACK; THE LINE DELTA IS ONE RECORD BACK. Ruling 1b: the word written
// at record i is consumed at the next fire and schedules the gap that LANDS record i+2. So the
// value is `this fire line - the previous fire line - 1` while the slot it goes into belongs to
// the record two back. Pairing the two-back slot with the two-back LINE is the mistake this design
// was drafted with and a lens sweep caught; the hand pin is the witness
// (games/sonic4/data/effects/ojz_effects.emp: arm0 = $8A00 | (99 - 1 - 1) = $8A61).
//
// PARK IS BY CONSTRUCTION. A record's arm is written only when a record two later is emitted, so
// the two youngest slots are never written by the loop — this proc parks them explicitly at the
// end. That is what keeps the TEMPLATE's own arm words entirely non-load-bearing: they are copied
// and then either overwritten or parked, so no patched template needs a special all-park emitter
// and check_arm_layout keeps proving what it proves for static programs. It also makes the empty
// schedule correct for the right reason: with every record suppressed the two youngest slots ARE
// the priming records, both parked.
//
// IT WRITES THE INACTIVE BUFFER AND SWAPS. Building in place would move records under a
// Raster_Cursor left from the previous frame. Today the buffer's LAYOUT is a frame invariant (only
// arm bytes change), so a stale cursor always points at a record boundary; under compaction it
// would not. Raster_Buf_A is free for the whole lifetime of a patched program, and P1 reserved the
// pair for exactly this.
//
// VBLANK ONLY, and that is inherited rather than new: the arm words are RELATIVE, so a half-built
// schedule walked by Raster_HInt desynchronises the whole tail of the chain.
//
// STACK SCRATCH, DELIBERATELY. The two arm-slot back-pointers live in an 8-byte stack frame rather
// than in registers or RAM: this proc runs under Raster_VBlank, whose callers VInt_Level and
// VInt_Lag declare clobbers reaching only d4 and a2, and Raster_BuildShipEntry's note records that
// saving a register does not buy back its declaration. Two longwords on the stack cost two memory
// accesses per record in VBlank and no contract change anywhere.
//
// Table entry (raster_dsl.emp's patch_table): [line_src][band_lo_fl][band_hi_fl][rec_off][rec_len],
// the first three in FIRE-LINE space, the last two byte offsets/lengths into the emitted body,
// preceded by a single word record count.
// -----------------------------------------------
proc Raster_BuildSchedule () clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a2) {
        move.l  Raster_Patch_Tab, d0
        beq     .none                       // liveness: the TABLE, never Active_Buf
        subq.l  #8, sp                      // (sp) = arm slot two back, 4(sp) = one back
        movea.l d0, a0                      // -> the patch table
        // --- destination = whichever buffer is NOT live ---
        move.l  Raster_Active_Buf, d3
        lea     Raster_Buf_A, a1
        cmpa.l  d3, a1
        bne     .dst_ready
        lea     Raster_Buf_B, a1
    .dst_ready:
        // --- header word and the two priming records, verbatim ---
        movea.l d0, a2
        // suba.w, not `lea -RASTER_BUF_SIZE(a2)`: a NEGATED named const in a displacement is
        // dropped by sigil's contract walk (measured — 2 dropped instructions, gate-fatal),
        // and suba.w #CONST,aN is this tree's existing idiom for the same step anyway.
        suba.w  #RASTER_BUF_SIZE, a2        // the template body sits before its table
        move.w  (a2)+, (a1)+                // pal_dirty_mask
        move.l  a1, (sp)                    // priming record 0's arm word
        move.l  (a2)+, (a1)+                // [arm][op_count]
        move.l  a1, 4(sp)                   // priming record 1's arm word
        move.l  (a2)+, (a1)+
        moveq   #1, d0                      // prev fire line = 1 (priming record 1)
        move.w  (a0)+, d4                   // record count (a WORD)
        subq.w  #1, d4
    .entry:
        move.w  (a0)+, d2                   // line_src
        bpl     .have_line                  // high bit clear -> a literal fire line
        andi.w  #RASTER_MAX_PATCH-1, d2     // channel index
        add.w   d2, d2                      // -> word offset
        lea     Effects_Screen_L, a2
        move.w  (a2, d2.w), d2              // the LATCHED screen line; may be negative
        subq.w  #1, d2                      // -> fire line. ONE conversion, here.
        cmp.w   2(a0), d2                   // band_hi_fl
        bgt     .suppress                   // past the band it can reach: REMOVE the record
        cmp.w   (a0), d2                    // band_lo_fl
        bge     .have_line
        move.w  (a0), d2                    // clamp UP — the frame-top ship covers what is above
    .have_line:
        addq.l  #4, a0                      // past the two band words
        // --- this record's gap goes into the slot TWO records back ---
        move.w  d2, d3
        sub.w   d0, d3
        subq.w  #1, d3                      // gap = L[k] - L[k-1] - 1
        movea.l (sp), a2
        move.b  d3, 1(a2)                   // the low byte IS the counter (every arm word is $8Axx)
        // --- shift the slot history, then remember where THIS record's arm lands ---
        move.l  4(sp), (sp)
        move.l  a1, 4(sp)
        move.w  d2, d0                      // prev = this record's fire line
        // --- copy the record body out of the template ---
        move.w  (a0)+, d3                   // rec_off
        movea.l Raster_Patch_Tab, a2
        suba.w  #RASTER_BUF_SIZE, a2
        adda.w  d3, a2                      // adda.w, NOT add.w: add.w dN,aM mis-encodes
        move.w  (a0)+, d3                   // rec_len, in BYTES
        lsr.w   #1, d3                      // -> words
        subq.w  #1, d3
    .copy:
        move.w  (a2)+, (a1)+
        dbf     d3, .copy
    .next:
        dbf     d4, .entry
        // --- terminator, emitted rather than copied: nothing in the template holds it ---
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_PARK, (a1)+
        move.w  #RASTER_OPS_END, (a1)+
        // --- the two youngest arm slots have no record two later, so they park ---
        movea.l (sp), a2
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_PARK, (a2)
        movea.l 4(sp), a2
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_PARK, (a2)
        addq.l  #8, sp
        // --- swap: the buffer just built becomes the one Raster_HInt walks ---
        move.l  Raster_Active_Buf, d3
        lea     Raster_Buf_A, a1
        cmpa.l  d3, a1
        bne     .swap
        lea     Raster_Buf_B, a1
    .swap:
        move.l  a1, Raster_Active_Buf
    .none:
        rts
    .suppress:
        addq.l  #8, a0                      // band words, rec_off, rec_len
        jbra    .next
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_GetChannelBand — report the fire-line band the LIVE patched program declares for
// a world-anchor channel, so a SECOND consumer of that anchor clamps exactly where
// Raster_BuildSchedule clamps it (:895-901).
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. Raster_BuildSchedule pins a patchable fire to its record's authored
// band, and that clamp is load-bearing rather than cosmetic: a negative inter-record gap
// would store $FF, which IS the park word, killing every remaining fire in the frame
// (see the note above that proc). Parcel W's parallax overlay reads the same anchor to
// place a scroll boundary. If it clamped anywhere else, then outside the record's band the
// palette boundary would pin while the scroll boundary kept following the camera — the two
// separating, which is the exact defect that parcel exists to remove. Not hypothetical:
// OJZ channel 0 is authored with band 3..120 and clamps at BOTH edges on the ordinary scroll
// path — to lo on descent, and to hi whenever the camera sits more than ~104 px above the
// water line (a plain jump from spawn reaches that). The band was 40..120 until the lo clamp
// was seen pinning the boundary 40 lines down; see the note at that `patchable` call.
//
// READING THE TABLE BEATS DUPLICATING THE BAND. The alternative was carrying the band in
// parallax data and keeping the two copies in step with a shared comptime const — still two
// authored numbers that agree only while every author remembers the const, which is the
// failure mode copy_band_entry's note preaches against. Here the clamp IS the raster
// record's own two words: change `patchable`'s band and both boundaries' clamp moves, with
// no second author action possible.
//
//   d0.w = channel
// Out: d0 = 0 -> no band; the caller must NOT clamp (see below). d0 = 1 -> d1.w = lo,
//      d2.w = hi, both FIRE lines (screen line - 1, :894's coordinate system).
//
// A ZERO RESULT IS A SEMANTIC ANSWER, NOT A FAILURE. Raster_Patch_Tab == 0 means no patched
// program is live — the anchored-parallax-without-a-patched-program case W0 legitimised — so
// there is no palette boundary to diverge from and an unclamped [0,224] is correct. Same for
// a channel with no patchable record in the live table.
//
// d1/d2 ARE ONLY MEANINGFUL WHEN d0 = 1. They are declared `out` unconditionally because
// that is what the register contract can express; the d0 test is the caller's obligation.
//
// CLOBBERS a0, which the parallax caller holds its config pointer in; that caller saves it.
// -----------------------------------------------
// THE OUTS ARE TYPED, and that is required rather than decorative: a BARE `out(dN)` claims
// all 32 bits, which only a `.l` write or a `moveq` satisfies, while the band words arrive
// here through `move.w`. `out(dN: u16)` claims exactly the low word — the width these values
// actually have — and sigil's out-verifier checks it at that width on every return path.
pub proc Raster_GetChannelBand (d0: u16) clobbers(a0) out(d0: u16, d1: u16, d2: u16) {
        // CAP_ANCHORS span (P2 §3.3), MODULE depth. This proc exists for ONE consumer:
        // the world-anchored parallax overlay, which reads the same anchor the palette
        // boundary does so the two clamp identically. A game with no anchored scene
        // elides that overlay, and with it the only engine call site — the table walk
        // below is then answering a question nobody asks.
        //
        // The else arm is the SAME semantic answer the null-table path gives (d0 = 0,
        // "no band, do not clamp"), not a degraded one: with no patch channels driving
        // a boundary there is genuinely no band to report. It is kept rather than
        // deleted because a proc header cannot sit under a comptime `if` in .emp
        // (measured: `expected a declaration, found Ident("if")`), and because sonic4's
        // ojz_scroll_test calls this from GAME code.
    if (Game.SCANLINE_CAPS & CAP_ANCHORS) != 0 {
    .cap_anchors_band_begin:
        move.l  Raster_Patch_Tab, d1
        beq     .none                       // liveness: the TABLE, exactly as Raster_BuildSchedule
        movea.l d1, a0
        andi.w  #RASTER_MAX_PATCH-1, d0
        ori.w   #$8000, d0                  // a patchable line_src is $8000|channel
        move.w  (a0)+, d2                   // record count (a WORD)
        beq     .none                       // malformed: raster_dsl ensures fires.len >= 1,
                                            // so this is unreachable — but a 0 here would
                                            // dbf 65536 times through ROM comparing garbage
        subq.w  #1, d2
    .entry:
        move.w  (a0)+, d1                   // line_src — the FIRST word of an entry now
        cmp.w   d0, d1
        beq     .found
        addq.l  #8, a0                      // skip band_lo, band_hi, rec_off, rec_len
        dbf     d2, .entry
    .none:
        // d1/d2 are zeroed on this path, not left as they fell, because `out(d1, d2)` is a
        // TOTAL contract: the null-table entry never touches d2 and sigil's out-verifier
        // says so (proc.out-unverified). Zeroing costs two instructions and makes the
        // declaration true on every return path rather than on the interesting one.
        moveq   #0, d0
        moveq   #0, d1
        moveq   #0, d2
        rts
    .found:
        move.w  (a0)+, d1                   // band_lo_fl
        move.w  (a0), d2                    // band_hi_fl
        moveq   #1, d0
        rts
    .cap_anchors_band_end:
    } else {
        moveq   #0, d0
        moveq   #0, d1
        moveq   #0, d2
        rts
    }
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Effects_LatchWorldLines — derive every patch channel's screen line from its world anchor,
// ONCE per frame, and store it unclamped.
//
//   Effects_Screen_L[ch] = Effects_World_Y[ch] - Camera_Y      (signed, may be off-screen)
//
// CALLED FROM THE MAIN LOOP, after Camera_Update and before Parallax_Update — and that
// placement is a ruling, not a convenience. Put it in VBlank instead and the value reaches the
// parallax overlay one Camera_Update LATER than it reaches Raster_BuildSchedule: the palette would
// go whole-screen wet on the frame the shimmer still splits mid-screen, a guaranteed one-frame
// pop at every transition. Verified ordering today has both consumers reading the same tick's
// camera, and this latch is what keeps that true rather than re-deriving it three times.
//
// THAT IS THE OTHER HALF OF WHY IT EXISTS: it is the SINGLE derivation of L. Raster_BuildSchedule
// (VBlank), the parallax overlay (main loop) and the frame-top ship (VBlank) all read this
// bank. On a VInt_Lag frame VBlank runs twice against one main-loop update, so three
// independent `anchor - Camera_Y` computations would put the fire line and the boundary state
// on different cameras.
//
// UNCLAMPED, DELIBERATELY. Raster_BuildSchedule clamps its fire line into each record's authored
// band and must (a negative inter-record gap stores as $FF, which IS the park word, and would
// kill every later fire in the frame) — but the clamp therefore cannot express "this boundary
// is above the screen entirely", which is precisely the state the off-screen ship answers.
// Consumers clamp for their own purposes; the bank holds the truth.
// -----------------------------------------------
// THE ANCHOR MOVER LIVES HERE, INSIDE THIS LOOP, AND THAT IS FORCED (EFFECTS-W1 DoD item 4).
// The paragraph above is the argument: three consumers read one derivation so that they read
// ONE CAMERA. A mover anywhere else re-opens exactly that — the raster fire would move on the
// frame the parallax split had not yet, which is the pop this proc exists to prevent — so the
// two motion terms are loop-body work in the walk that already exists, not a second walk and
// not a second call site. The design ruled out four alternatives (a write-back accumulator on
// the anchor, a scene-level clock the consumers each index, a mover on the band record, and
// Batman & Robin's "the boundary IS an object's world Y"); the reasons are in
// docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-02-moving-bands-anchor-mover-design.md §4.3.
//
//   Effects_World_Y[ch]  <- approach(anchor, Target[ch], RateMask[ch], tick)      APPROACH
//   Effects_Screen_L[ch] <- Effects_World_Y[ch] + sweep(Motion[ch], tick) - Camera_Y   SWEEP
//
// THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. APPROACH publishes into the anchor bank, so gameplay reading
// Effects_World_Y sees a real world Y; SWEEP does NOT, because it is a display displacement
// and an anchor has exactly one writer. The consequence is stated rather than discovered: a
// future consumer asking "is the player under the waterline" reads the MEAN, not the swept
// surface. S.C.E. publishes both (Water_level vs Mean_water_level) and its gameplay reads the
// displayed one; the fix here, when something needs it, is an Effects_Display_Y[4] bank and
// one move.w per channel. Nothing reads the bank today, so it costs nothing today.
//
// THE CAPABILITY GATE AND THE MOTION_ANY GATE ARE TWO DIFFERENT GATES doing two different
// jobs. The capability elides the whole block from a game that authors no motion at all (demo
// pays zero bytes). Effects_Motion_Any elides it per-FRAME in a game that does, on every
// section that authors none: one `tst.w` + `beq` for 12 cycles, skipping 224 to 896.
//
// THIS INVERTS BAND DRIFT'S RULE ON PURPOSE, and the inversion is the point rather than an
// inconsistency: BAND-DRIFT §3.1 refuses a per-band test on the rate because "a zero rate
// costs 48 cycles; testing for it costs ~30 to save ~40" — 0.75:1. Here the same test is
// 19:1 to 75:1. The rule both decisions obey is "measure the ratio", never "always test" or
// "never test".
//
// A GAME THAT DECLARES THE BIT AND AUTHORS NOTHING RUNS THE OLD LOOP UNCHANGED, plus the 12.
pub proc Effects_LatchWorldLines () clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a2) {
        move.w  Camera_Y, d1                // 16.16 -> the integer word
        lea     Effects_World_Y, a0
        moveq   #RASTER_MAX_PATCH-1, d0
    if (Game.SCANLINE_CAPS & CAP_ANCHOR_MOTION) != 0 {
    .cap_anchor_motion_latch_begin:
        // THE ONCE-PER-FRAME GATE. Everything below it is skipped on a section that authors
        // no motion, which is every section but one today.
        tst.w   Effects_Motion_Any          // (Effects_Motion_Any).w
        beq     .plain                      // nothing authored: fall into the shipped loop
        // ONE CURSOR OVER THREE BANKS. Effects_Motion, Effects_Target and Effects_RateMask are
        // three adjacent [u16; RASTER_MAX_PATCH] arrays, so `(a2)`, `8(a2)` and `16(a2)` reach
        // all three and one post-increment advances all three. That is not a micro-optimisation
        // — it is what keeps this proc inside the register budget its CALLERS already have:
        // three separate `lea`s needed a3, which Effects_InstallPreset holds the EffectsPreset*
        // in across its call to this proc, and sigil's [call.live-clobbered] closure said so
        // (measured, this parcel). The adjacency is pinned by the `ensure`s beside
        // RASTER_STATE_SIZE above; break it and the build fails there, not here.
        lea     Effects_Motion, a2
        // `lea` FIRST, AND NOT `Sine_Table(pc,dN.w)`. The 68000's PC-relative INDEXED mode
        // carries an 8-bit displacement, so a cross-section indexed read of a ROM table has to
        // load the base into an address register whatever the distance happens to be — the
        // reach is 128 bytes, not 32 KB. engine/level/parallax.emp measured the link failure
        // (`(d8,PC,Xn) displacement to Sine_Table out of range (-20794)`) copying the spelling
        // engine/system/math.emp uses thirty bytes from the table.
        //
        // a1 IS THE SINE BASE HERE AND THE SCREEN BANK IS REACHED BY DISPLACEMENT INSTEAD:
        // Effects_Screen_L sits exactly 2 * RASTER_MAX_PATCH bytes after Effects_World_Y, so
        // after the post-incremented anchor read below, `6(a0)` IS this channel's screen slot.
        // Same reason as the shared cursor — one fewer address register, and the offset is
        // pinned by an `ensure` rather than assumed.
        lea     Sine_Table, a1
    .mch:
        // ---- APPROACH: a rate-gated ONE-PIXEL ramp of the anchor toward its target ----
        move.w  8(a2), d4                   // target world Y; PATCH_ANCHOR_NONE = no ramp
        cmpi.w  #PATCH_ANCHOR_NONE, d4
        beq     .no_ramp
        move.w  Logic_Tick+2, d3            // the LOW WORD — the lag-immune tick
        and.w   16(a2), d3                  // THE RATE GATE: (1 << rate_shift) - 1
        bne     .no_ramp                    // shut on this frame
        sub.w   (a0), d4                    // d4 = target - anchor
        beq     .no_ramp                    // arrived. Terminal, forever, for one compare
        bmi     .ramp_up                    // target above the anchor -> world Y decreases
        addq.w  #1, (a0)
        jbra    .no_ramp
    .ramp_up:
        subq.w  #1, (a0)
        // ONE PIXEL, NEVER `add.w speed`. S.C.E. (Water Effects.asm:49-70) and stock S2
        // (s2.asm:5343-5365) both add a speed byte with no clamp to the remaining distance, so
        // a distance that is not a multiple of the speed oscillates by +/-speed forever. It
        // never bites in either tree only because their speed is 1 and is written exactly
        // once. A one-pixel step is their speed-1 case made universal, with the rate moved
        // into the gate — which is what makes overshoot unreachable rather than merely fixed.
    .no_ramp:
        // ---- SWEEP: stateless, periodic, added to the SCREEN LINE and not to the anchor ----
        // It is computed FIRST, into the accumulator, so d2 is a third scratch register while
        // the anchor is still in memory. Adding the anchor last costs nothing (`add.w (a0)+`
        // where the plain loop has `move.w (a0)+`) and buys the register.
        moveq   #0, d2
        move.w  (a2)+, d4                   // packed (a << 12) | (p << 8) | ph; 0 = static
        beq     .no_sweep
        move.w  d4, d2
        lsr.w   #8, d2
        and.w   #$0F, d2                    // d2 = period shift p
        move.w  Logic_Tick+2, d3
        lsr.w   d2, d3                      // tick >> p
        // THE PHASE COSTS NOTHING. Adding the WHOLE packed word is the same as adding the
        // phase byte, because the mask below keeps eight bits and the two nibbles live above
        // them — so the per-channel phase offset Ristar's shared wave table needs is one
        // `add.w` that was going to happen anyway.
        add.w   d4, d3
        and.w   #SINE_CYCLE_ENTRIES-1, d3   // one full cycle; never the $40 overlap
        add.w   d3, d3                      // the table's entries are WORDS
        move.w  (a1,d3.w), d2               // -SINE_AMPLITUDE .. +SINE_AMPLITUDE
        rol.w   #4, d4                      // amplitude shift a into the low nibble
        and.w   #$0F, d4
        asr.w   d4, d2                      // ARITHMETIC: the wave is signed
    .no_sweep:
        add.w   (a0)+, d2                   // + the anchor (which APPROACH may just have moved)
        sub.w   d1, d2                      // may go negative, meaningfully
        move.w  d2, 6(a0)                   // Effects_Screen_L[ch] — see the a1 note above
        dbf     d0, .mch
        rts
    .cap_anchor_motion_latch_end:
    }
    .plain:
        lea     Effects_Screen_L, a1
    .plain_ch:
        move.w  (a0)+, d2
        sub.w   d1, d2                      // may go negative, meaningfully
        move.w  d2, (a1)+
        dbf     d0, .plain_ch
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Effects_SetTargetY — arm (or disarm) an APPROACH ramp on one patch channel.
//
// THE RETARGET HANDLE, mirroring Effects_SetWorldY instruction for instruction: that proc is
// the TELEPORT (it moves the position; the ramp then continues from wherever it lands), this
// one is the ramp. S.C.E. spends the sign bit of a Y word on exactly that snap-vs-ramp
// distinction (Check Range.asm:562-577); we get the same expressiveness for free from a proc
// that already ships.
//
//   d0.w = channel   d1.w = target world Y (PATCH_ANCHOR_NONE disarms)   d2.w = rate shift
//
// THE RATE SHIFT, NOT A MASK, is the parameter — the units an author reads on the ladder in
// raster_dsl.emp's anchor_rate_mask. The conversion is three instructions on a proc called
// once per event, and it keeps a runtime-chosen rate expressible. `lsl.w` with a register
// count of 16 yields 0, so shift 16 lands the full $FFFF mask correctly rather than wrapping.
//
// OWNERSHIP RULE, STATED ONCE: a channel with a live target is owned by the mover; a channel
// with PATCH_ANCHOR_NONE is owned by whoever writes Effects_World_Y. Both are legal. Both at
// once is an authoring error the engine cannot detect and does not try to.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Effects_SetTargetY (d0: u16, d1: u16, d2: u16) clobbers(d0-d2/a0) {
    if (Game.SCANLINE_CAPS & CAP_ANCHOR_MOTION) != 0 {
    .cap_anchor_motion_target_begin:
        andi.w  #RASTER_MAX_PATCH-1, d0
        add.w   d0, d0
        lea     Effects_Target, a0
        move.w  d1, (a0, d0.w)
        moveq   #1, d1
        lsl.w   d2, d1
        subq.w  #1, d1                      // (1 << rate_shift) - 1
        lea     Effects_RateMask, a0
        move.w  d1, (a0, d0.w)
        // Arm the once-per-frame gate. It is an OR and not a store because the word already
        // carries the install-time fold of the four sweep words, and a ramp must not clear
        // them. Any nonzero value means "walk the channels"; the bits carry no other meaning.
        // Disarming a ramp deliberately does NOT clear it — the next preset install recomputes
        // the word from scratch, and until then a spuriously-armed gate costs the 12-cycle
        // test plus four inactive arms, while a spuriously-CLEARED one would stop a sweep the
        // section really authored.
        ori.w   #1, Effects_Motion_Any
    .cap_anchor_motion_target_end:
    }
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Effects_SetWorldY — move a patch channel's world anchor. THE named handle for every
// runtime-varying effect: rising lava, a flood line, a beat-driven pulse. Without it the only
// way to move a boundary is poking RAM at an index the author has to guess.
//   d0.w = channel   d1.w = world Y
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Effects_SetWorldY (d0: u16, d1: u16) clobbers(d0/a0) {
        andi.w  #RASTER_MAX_PATCH-1, d0
        add.w   d0, d0
        lea     Effects_World_Y, a0
        move.w  d1, (a0, d0.w)
        rts
}

